ed\", \"TarArchive\", \"WalkResult\"]  def _write_archive(     out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *, cancel: threading.Event \\| None = None ) -> None: ...  def _write_archive_system_tar(     out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *, cancel: threading.Event \\| None = None ) -> str \\| None: ...  # TarArchive static helper, with the existing result shape: def _archive_result(     label: str, arcbase: Path, out: Path, walk: WalkResult, engine: str,     *, cancel: threading.Event \\| None = None, ) -> StepResult: ... ```  `ArchiveCancelled` is the PRD-required exception at the archive/runner boundary. It carries no new state. It must escape both writer helpers and the capability without triggering fallback. The runner converts it to exactly `StepResult(name, success=False, message=\"cancelled\")`, with `archive=None` and `skipped=False`.  `TarArchive.run(self, **kwargs: object) -> Iterator[StepResult]` accepts an optional runtime `cancel` kwarg holding `threading.Event`. Omitting it preserves direct-call behavior. `_run_validated` forwards it to `_archive_result`; the latter passes the same event to both the selected engine and any legitimate Python fallback. Check a supplied event before selection starts, after selection, and before creating archive output. An already-cancelled call must not walk or write. No `cancel` entry is added to `TarArchive.inputs`.  The Python writer checks `cancel.is_set()` before every `tar.add`, after the member loop, and immediately before `os.replace`. A set event raises `ArchiveCancelled`. Keep the existing `except BaseException` temp cleanup, streaming compression, `fsync`, mode `0o600`, and atomic replacement. Cancellation during a single large member takes effect when that `tar.add` returns; v1 does not interrupt a file halfway through its copy. A final event check also covers an empty member iterable.  The system writer preserves the exact argv, NUL-delimited bytes input, `COPYFILE_DISABLE=1`, return-reason fallback behavior, permissions, and atomic publication. Start with `subprocess.Popen(..., stdin=subprocess.PIPE, stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, env=env, start_new_session=True)`. A private session prevents terminal Ctrl-C from killing tar independently of the runner's cancellation decision; this child needs no terminal input.  Call `process.communicate(input=filelist, timeout=0.2)` once. After `subprocess.TimeoutExpired`, retry `process.communicate(timeout=0.2)` without `input`. Check the event before spawn, between retries, after communicate succeeds, and immediately before publication. A timeout is a polling interval, not a failed archive or a fallback reason.  On cancellation while the child is alive: call `process.terminate()`, drain/reap with `process.communicate(timeout=5.0)`, then on `TimeoutExpired` call `process.kill()` and `process.communicate()` to reap it. Suppress only an already-exited-child race when signaling. Close owned pipes and remove the temp in cleanup. Any other exception after spawn must also stop/reap the live child before propagating or returning the existing invocation-failure fallback reason. Cancellation remains `ArchiveCancelled`, including cancellation during cleanup; it must not become a Python fallback. A child already exited still requires the event check before publication.  Publication is the existing `os.replace` operation. An archive published before cancellation is completed and is preserved. An observed cancellation before publication prevents replacement; the old output stays byte-identical, and a previously absent output stays absent. The immediately preceding check is cooperative, not a transactional synchronization primitive with arbitrary external event setters.    Details: - Implement only this feature slice. - Include its release note in CHANGELOG.md when listed; do not alter prior notes. - Tests belong to tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py, with sibling test modules if needed; required PRD-named