ring an interrupt while rendering the final summary. A new run resets the state as specified above. The method performs no rendering, yielding, joining, or exception raising. Both the runner's own KeyboardInterrupt handler and a consumer whose rendering was interrupted use this method.  Each run creates its own event named `cancel = threading.Event()`, completion queue `queue.Queue[Future[list[StepResult]]]`, and `ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self._jobs)`. No executor is needed for an empty selection or a selection containing only preflight failures. Preserve `_plan(selected, now)` as the preflight step, before any submission; preflight `FatalError` retains its existing propagation contract. Preflight failure rows yield their existing failed results from the calling thread and are never submitted. Do not turn a preflight `skip` into a new failure; retain the capability's existing skip behavior.  Sort execution candidates by `(instance.order, name)` and submit in that order. Maintain at most `jobs` submitted, uncollected futures, and refill from the remaining candidates after each completion. This keeps the scheduling queue bounded and leaves not-yet-started instances under calling-thread ownership. With `jobs=1`, capability execution is strictly sequential. With larger values, ordering is submit priority; worker-start and completion order can differ.  `_run_instance` checks `cancel` before invoking the capability and builds a fresh kwargs dictionary with fresh excludes. Pass `label`, `excludes`, `dry_run`, `now`, and `cancel` after `instance.with_`, so runtime values win. Fully consume the capability iterator inside the worker and return its results as a list. Workers never yield to the outside caller or render output. Registered capabilities are shared, stateless objects; per-call mutation belongs in local variables.  Catch `ArchiveCancelled` first for the exact cancelled result. Catch other `Exception`, including execution-time `FatalError`, as a failed result w