onsumer code, including `_report_step`, counter rendering, and final summary rendering: a suspended generator cannot catch those exceptions.  With `on_interrupt` supplied, `_report_steps` catches `KeyboardInterrupt` around consuming and rendering each step and its summary, calls `on_interrupt()`, and resumes consumption of the same iterator until exhaustion. Keep any fetched-but-not-fully-rendered step pending and retry its render; count each step once. Keep the counters across retries. An interrupt must not discard the generator, invoke `close()` as the cancellation path, or restart a run. Resuming it lets the runner observe the event, stop refill, join active writers, and yield cancelled outcomes. Already-completed/published outcomes stay successful even if their rendering was interrupted. Repeated interrupts repeat the idempotent cancellation request and continue draining. If no callback is supplied, preserve the helper's existing KeyboardInterrupt propagation behavior. Use `finally` in `_run_plan` to close the retained generator on an unrelated consumer exception, invoking the runner's resource-cleanup guarantee.  After `_report_steps` finishes, if `runner.interrupted`, print `console.failure(\"interrupted: \" + \", \".join(runner.cancelled_names))`; use `\"interrupted: no unfinished instances\"` when the tuple is empty. Catch KeyboardInterrupt during this final interruption message, request cancellation again, and retry the message before `raise SystemExit(130)`. An interrupt during the ordinary final summary also uses the callback above, so it reaches this exit-130 decision. Otherwise return the existing failure boolean and retain exit 1 for ordinary failures/unknown selectors, exit 0 for success. Workers have no access to the console.   Details: - Implement only this feature slice. - Include its release note in CHANGELOG.md when listed; do not alter prior notes. - Tests belong to tests/tools/backup/test_parallel_runner.py, with sibling test modules if needed;