+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T20:42:43.735Z","ordinal":32,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a198d9-52a2-7b63-b71b-a496f3202e83","turn_id":"01a198d9-52cd-7300-bac5-f48f3b1e760b","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-63a6eb32-4e13-4a35-852c-55cc84010ba6","process_id":"40557","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","python3 -c 'import subprocess; subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-c\", \"for path in AGENTS.md src/AGENTS.md src/tools/AGENTS.md src/tools/backup/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \\\"$path\\\" ]; then rg -n \\\"^\\\" \\\"$path\\\"; fi; done\"], timeout=60, check=True)'"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"python3 -c 'import subprocess; subprocess.run([\"bash\", \"-c\", \"for path in AGENTS.md src/AGENTS.md src/tools/AGENTS.md src/tools/backup/AGENTS.md; do if [ -f \\\"$path\\\" ]; then rg -n \\\"^\\\" \\\"$path\\\"; fi; done\"], timeout=60, check=True)'"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"1:# AGENTS.md\n2:\n3:BUVIS gems monorepo. Python toolkit (buvis-pybase + zettel) and CLI tools, shipped as a single PyPI package `buvis-gems`. Supports Python 3.11+.\n4:\n5:## Reading these instructions\n6:\n7:This root `AGENTS.md` is the always-on canonical guidance. Some directories also\n8:carry their own nested `AGENTS.md` with rules scoped to the code beside them.\n9:Before working on files in a directory, discover and read any `AGENTS.md` on the\n10:path from the repo root down to that directory, and follow it for files in its\n11:scope. Do this yourself even if your host did not load those files automatically\n12:— host loading of nested files varies and is not guaranteed (see\n13:[`docs/dev/architecture/agent-host-support.md`](docs/dev/architecture/agent-host-support.md)).\n14:\n15:## Quick Start\n16:\n17:```bash\n18:uv sync --all-groups --all-extras            # install deps\n19:pre-commit install                          # setup hooks\n20:uv run pytest                               # run tests\n21:uv run pytest -m snapshot                   # run snapshot tests only (skipped off canonical env)\n22:uv run pytest --snapshot-update             # regenerate snapshot baselines (local use only)\n23:uv run mypy src/lib/ src/tools/              # type check\n24:uv run sphinx-build -b html docs/source docs/build/html  # build docs\n25:```\n26:\n27:## Architecture\n28:\n29:```text\n30:src/\n31:├── lib/buvis/pybase/           # shared library (namespace pkg: no buvis/__init__.py)\n32:│   ├── adapters/               # jira, console, config adapters\n33:│   ├── configuration/          # settings/config helpers\n34:│   ├── filesystem/             # file utilities\n35:│   ├── formatting/             # output formatting\n36:│   └── zettel/                 # zettel subsystem\n37:│       ├── domain/             # entities, services, interfaces, value objects\n38:│       ├── application/        # use cases\n39:│       ├── infrastructure/     # formatters, repositories, file parsers\n40:│       └── integrations/       # jira assemblers\n41:└── tools/\n42:    ├── bim/                    # BUVIS InfoMesh CLI (zettel integration)\n43:    ├── dot/                    # dotfiles manager\n44:    ├── fctracker/              # foreign currency tracker\n45:    ├── fren/                   # file renamer toolkit\n46:    ├── morph/                  # file conversion toolkit\n47:    ├── muc/                    # music collection tools\n48:    ├── netscan/                # network scanning tools\n49:    ├── outlookctl/             # Outlook CLI\n50:    ├── pinger/                 # ICMP ping utilities\n51:    ├── puc/                    # photo utility collection\n52:    ├── readerctl/              # Readwise Reader CLI\n53:    ├── sysup/                  # system update tools\n54:    ├── vuc/                    # video utility collection\n55:    └── zseq/                   # Zettelsequence utilities\n56:tests/\n57:├── lib/                        # library tests\n58:│   ├── pybase/\n59:│   ├── zettel/\n60:│   └── zettel_integrations/\n61:└── tools/                      # CLI tool tests\n62:tools/                          # Dev command shims (on PATH via mise) + helpers\n63:├── release                     # public shim → mise `release` task\n64:└── lib/                        # helpers, invoked by path (NOT on PATH)\n65:    ├── release                 # release impl: bump+tag+push\n66:    ├── pin_deps.py             # pin deps from uv.lock for publishing\n67:    ├── scaffold.py             # scaffold a new tool\n68:    ├── check_audit_ignores.py  # pip-audit suppression guard (+ audit/ config)\n69:    ├── check_tool_wiring.py    # pre-commit tool-wiring check\n70:    ├── parity_brief_portfolio.py\n71:    └── gen_zettel_writer_fixtures.py\n72:```\n73:\n74:**Key patterns:**\n75:\n76:- **Single package**: `buvis-gems` on PyPI, one `uv tool install`\n77:- **Namespace package**: `src/lib/buvis/` has no `__init__.py`\n78:- **Hatch packages**: each `src/tools/<name>/` maps to top-level `<name>` in wheel\n79:- **Libraries are internal**: pybase + zettel not published separately\n80:- **All-interface rule**: every command/action must work across CLI, TUI, API, and WebUI\n81:\n82:## Tool Structure\n83:\n84:Every tool under `src/tools/<name>/` follows a base layout:\n85:\n86:```text\n87:tool_name/\n88:├── __init__.py\n89:├── __main__.py\n90:├── cli.py              # Click entry point\n91:├── settings.py         # Tool-specific settings\n92:└── commands/           # One module per CLI command\n93:```\n94:\n95:Add subdirs only when needed:\n96:\n97:| Dir | When | Example |\n98:|-----|------|---------|\n99:| `adapters/` | External service clients | fctracker, readerctl |\n100:| `domain/` | Business logic beyond simple commands | fctracker |\n101:| `shared/` | Code reused across commands | zseq |\n102:\n103:> `bim/commands/serve/frontend/` contains the WebUI SvelteKit app — the only tool with a frontend subtree.\n104:\n105:> `bim/commands/doc/` follows the existing `commands/serve/`-style group pattern with a `shared/` subdir holding cross-command utilities (pipeline, OCR/classifier/extractor adapters, state DB, issuer registry, zettel writer).\n106:\n107:## Code Conventions\n108:\n109:**Type hints** — modern style, no `Optional`:\n110:\n111:```python\n112:from __future__ import annotations\n113:def foo(path: Path | None = None) -> list[str]: ...\n114:```\n115:\n116:**Imports**:\n117:\n118:- Explicit `__all__` in `__init__.py`\n119:- `TYPE_CHECKING` guards for type-only imports\n120:\n121:**Docstrings**: Google format\n122:\n123:**CLI pattern**: Click-based. `buvis_options` adds `--version`, `--config`, `--log-level`, `--debug`, and `--update` to every tool automatically:\n124:\n125:```python\n126:@click.command()\n127:@buvis_options\n128:@click.pass_context\n129:def cli(ctx: click.Context) -> None:\n130:    settings = get_settings(ctx)\n131:    ...\n132:```\n133:\n134:**Lazy imports in CLI handlers**: import command classes inside handler functions, not at module level. This avoids pulling in optional/heavy dependencies before the user picks a subcommand:\n135:\n136:```python\n137:@cli.command(\"create\")\n138:@click.pass_context\n139:def create(ctx):\n140:    from bim.commands.create_note.create_note import CommandCreateNote\n141:    ...\n142:```\n143:\n144:## Error Handling\n145:\n146:**Command layer** (`commands/`):\n147:- Return `CommandResult` for all outcomes — success and failure\n148:- Catch expected exceptions (e.g. `FileNotFoundError`) internally, return `CommandResult(success=False, error=str(exc))`\n149:- No `console.panic()` or `sys.exit()` inside command classes\n150:- No custom exception classes for control flow\n151:\n152:**CLI layer** (`cli.py`):\n153:- Inspect `CommandResult` and call `console.panic()` / `console.failure()` / `console.success()`\n154:- Use `console.report_result()` for standardized output\n155:- Catch `ImportError` for optional deps, call `console.require_import()`\n156:- Catch `FatalError` for unrecoverable infrastructure failures\n157:\n158:**Click argument validation**:\n159:- Avoid parse-time validators that short-circuit before the callback (`click.Path(exists=True)`, `IntRange`, custom `ParamType.convert` that raises). They emit Click's default `Usage: ... Error: ...` formatting and bypass the buvis console.\n160:- Validate inside the callback instead: `if not path.is_file(): console.panic(f\"file not found: {path}\"); return`.\n161:- `click.Choice` is the exception — its parse-time error lists the valid values, which is more useful than a rewritten message.\n162:\n163:**Console methods**:\n164:- Fatal (exit): `console.panic(msg)` — prints + exits with code 1\n165:- Recoverable: `console.failure(msg)` — prints, continues\n166:- Status: `console.success()`, `console.warning()`, `console.info()`\n167:- All user-facing output must route through `console`. No `print()`, `sys.stdout.write()`, `sys.stderr.write()`, `click.echo()`, `click.secho()`, or the Python logging module. Plain writes bypass the buvis prefix markers (`✘`, `✓`, etc.) and become indistinguishable from stray output, which is what makes a missed `console.panic` look like a print statement to the user.\n168:- The one sanctioned exception is the `dot filter` clean/smudge plumbing, which writes git's blob payload as raw bytes on stdout and at most one error line on stderr, because git owns both streams and a console marker would corrupt the payload (discovery R12).\n169:- Never let raw exceptions (stack traces) reach the user.\n170:\n171:## Invariants (evolution guardrails)\n172:\n173:These encode the failure classes surfaced by the 2026-07-09 evolution assessment (`docs/dev/project-management/reviews/evolution-assessment-2026-07-09.md`). Each is either **HOLDS** (enforce it) or a **GAP** (a tracked PRD is closing it — write new code to the target state, not the current one).\n174:\n175:- **Atomic persistence** — never persist a note, state file, or config with a bare `Path.write_text`/`open(...,\"w\")`. Use `pybase.filesystem.atomic_write` (tempfile + fsync + `os.replace`). Truncate-then-write loses data on crash/ENOSPC. *HOLDS for the library and note/updater write paths (00041) — keep it, except `ConfigWriter.write` (`configuration/config_writer.py`), which still does a bare `write_text` — deliberately out of scope for 00041. GAP → pidash was closed by retiring pidash (00071); autopilot-state durability now lives in the buvis home repo (tracon/statectl), not gems.*\n176:- **Confine request-derived paths** — any filesystem path built from an HTTP request / external input must be `resolve()`d and asserted under an allowed root before read/write/delete/open. Network-facing servers must have auth + `TrustedHostMiddleware`. *HOLDS (00042) — bim serve confines request paths via `confine_path` (403 outside the vault/archive roots), mints a startup token required as `X-Buvis-Token`, installs `TrustedHostMiddleware`, and warns on a non-loopback bind (`commands/serve/_security.py`). Keep it.*\n177:- **The library stays interface-agnostic** — `src/lib/buvis/pybase/` must not import a UI framework at module import time or emit output outside the console adapter. No import-time Click monkey-patching, no `sys.exit`/`click.echo`/`print` in lib code. A TUI/API/WebUI process must be able to `import` settings without pulling Click. *HOLDS (00052) — keep it.*\n178:- **One action, one implementation across interfaces** — CLI/TUI/API/WebUI adapters must drive the same command class / use case through the composition root and return `CommandResult`; do not reimplement business logic per interface (dot's TUI is the anti-pattern; bim's serve action registry is the exemplar). *HOLDS (00051 design → 00053/00054) — the all-interface seam design (00051) is realized: dot's git ops unified behind `DotGitService` (00053) and bim's serve/TUI converged on the shared action registry (00054). Keep it.*\n179:- **Claim/lifecycle release on any exit** — resource claims and locks release in `finally` (or catch `BaseException`), never only `except Exception`; `KeyboardInterrupt` must not leak a claim. *HOLDS (00044) — the bim doc claim/dedup pipeline releases claims and locks in `finally` (`commands/doc/shared/`); keep it.*\n180:- **`CommandResult` discipline** — commands return `CommandResult`; the CLI/adapter layer renders it (`console.report_result`) and maps exit/HTTP status. No `sys.exit`/`console.panic` inside command classes. *HOLDS in `src/tools/*/commands/**` — keep it.*\n181:- **Clean-architecture layering + tool isolation** — zettel `domain → application → infrastructure → integrations` one-directional; no cross-tool imports; no `src/lib → src/tools`. *HOLDS — keep it.*\n182:\n183:## Testing\n184:\n185:- pytest + pytest-mock + pytest-cov\n186:- Tests in `tests/` mirror `src/lib/` and `src/tools/` structure\n187:- Mock subprocess calls heavily\n188:- Class-based test organization\n189:- **No unused imports/variables**\n190:- **Markers**: `pytest -m <tool>` runs one tool, `pytest -m lib` runs library tests, `pytest -m \"not bim\"` excludes a tool. Auto-applied by path in `tests/conftest.py` — no decorators needed. Scaffold registers markers for new tools.\n191:- **Snapshot tests** (`@pytest.mark.snapshot`) only run on the canonical env (Linux + Python 3.12) — SVG output from Rich/Textual differs across OS/Python so baselines must come from one place. Anywhere else they are auto-skipped. To regenerate baselines run the `update-snapshots` GitHub workflow (`gh workflow run update-snapshots.yml`); it runs `pytest --snapshot-update` on the canonical env and commits the result. Force locally with `BUVIS_SNAPSHOT_CANONICAL=1` only if you know what you're doing.\n192:\n193:## Installation\n194:\n195:```bash\n196:uv tool install buvis-gems              # core only (no tool-specific deps)\n197:uv tool install buvis-gems[bim]         # with jira support\n198:uv tool install buvis-gems[bim-web]     # bim web UI deps\n199:uv tool install buvis-gems[all]         # everything\n200:uv tool upgrade buvis-gems              # update\n201:```\n202:\n203:Extras: `bim`, `bim-web`, `doc`, `dot`, `fren`, `morph`, `muc`, `pinger`, `postup`, `postup-web`, `readerctl`, `all`\n204:\n205:## Release\n206:\n207:> Full procedure (version authority, checks, destinations, partial-failure recovery): `docs/dev/procedures/releasing.md`.\n208:\n209:```bash\n210:release patch|minor|major              # bump, tag, push → CI publishes to PyPI\n211:release --pre rc1                      # pre-release current version to TestPyPI\n212:release --pre rc1 minor                # bump + pre-release to TestPyPI\n213:release                                # after rc: strip suffix, release stable to PyPI\n214:release local                          # build .devN wheel + install locally\n215:release --dry-run [--pre rc1] [patch]  # preview without changes\n216:```\n217:\n218:`mise` adds `tools` to PATH (shims; `tools/lib` holds the implementations). Tags with `rc` in the name publish to TestPyPI; stable tags go to PyPI. Manual workflow dispatch defaults to TestPyPI.\n219:\n220:**First-time setup** (already done for buvis-gems):\n221:- test.pypi.org: add trusted publisher (owner: `buvis`, repo: `gems`, workflow: `publish.yml`, env: `testpypi`)\n222:- GitHub repo settings: create `testpypi` and `pypi` environments\n223:\n224:**Why explicit version in pyproject.toml?** maturin reads the version from `pyproject.toml` at build time to compile the Rust extension. Tag-based versioning (hatch-vcs) would require glue to inject the version before maturin sees it. Pure Python projects like mkdocs-zettelkasten use hatch-vcs instead — the version derives from the git tag, no file to keep in sync, no bump commits.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2750}},"started_at_ms":1793565763735,"completed_at_ms":1793565763735}}
