    ...\n844:```\n845:\n846:## Error Handling\n847:\n848:**Command layer** (`commands/`):\n849:- Return `CommandResult` for all outcomes — success and failure\n850:- Catch expected exceptions (e.g. `FileNotFoundError`) internally, return `CommandResult(success=False, error=str(exc))`\n851:- No `console.panic()` or `sys.exit()` inside command classes\n852:- No custom exception classes for control flow\n853:\n854:**CLI layer** (`cli.py`):\n855:- Inspect `CommandResult` and call `console.panic()` / `console.failure()` / `console.success()`\n856:- Use `console.report_result()` for standardized output\n857:- Catch `ImportError` for optional deps, call `console.require_import()`\n858:- Catch `FatalError` for unrecoverable infrastructure failures\n859:\n860:**Click argument validation**:\n861:- Avoid parse-time validators that short-circuit before the callback (`click.Path(exists=True)`, `IntRange`, custom `ParamType.convert` that raises). They emit Click's default `Usage: ... Error: ...` formatting and bypass the buvis console.\n862:- Validate inside the callback instead: `if not path.is_file(): console.panic(f\"file not found: {path}\"); return`.\n863:- `click.Choice` is the exception — its parse-time error lists the valid values, which is more useful than a rewritten message.\n864:\n865:**Console methods**:\n866:- Fatal (exit): `console.panic(msg)` — prints + exits with code 1\n867:- Recoverable: `console.failure(msg)` — prints, continues\n868:- Status: `console.success()`, `console.warning()`, `console.info()`\n869:- All user-facing output must route through `console`. No `print()`, `sys.stdout.write()`, `sys.stderr.write()`, `click.echo()`, `click.secho()`, or the Python logging module. Plain writes bypass the buvis prefix markers (`✘`, `✓`, etc.) and become indistinguishable from stray output, which is what makes a missed `console.panic` look like a print statement to the user.\n870:- The one sanctioned exception is the `dot filter` clean/smudge plumbing, which writes git's blob payload as raw bytes on stdout and at most one error line on stderr, because git owns both streams and a console marker would corrupt the payload (discovery R12).\n871:- Never let raw exceptions (stack traces) reach the user.\n872:\n873:## Invariants (evolution guardrails)\n874:\n875:These encode the failure classes surfaced by the 2026-07-09 evolution assessment (`docs/dev/project-management/reviews/evolution-assessment-2026-07-09.md`). Each is either **HOLDS** (enforce it) or a **GAP** (a tracked PRD is closing it — write new code to the target state, not the current one).\n876:\n877:- **Atomic persistence** — never persist a note, state file, or config with a bare `Path.write_text`/`open(...,\"w\")`. Use `pybase.filesystem.atomic_write` (tempfile + fsync + `os.replace`). Truncate-then-write loses data on crash/ENOSPC. *HOLDS for the library and note/updater write paths (00041) — keep it, except `ConfigWriter.write` (`configuration/config_writer.py`), which still does a bare `write_text` — deliberately out of scope for 00041. GAP → pidash was closed by retiring pidash (00071); autopilot-state durability now lives in the buvis home repo (tracon/statectl), not gems.*\n878:- **Confine request-derived paths** — any filesystem path built from an HTTP request / external input must be `resolve()`d and asserted under an allowed root before read/write/delete/open. Network-facing servers must have auth + `TrustedHostMiddleware`. *HOLDS (00042) — bim serve confines request paths via `confine_path` (403 outside the vault/archive roots), mints a startup token required as `X-Buvis-Token`, installs `TrustedHostMiddleware`, and warns on a non-loopback bind (`commands/serve/_security.py`). Keep it.*\n879:- **The library stays interface-agnostic** — `src/lib/buvis/pybase/` must not import a UI framework at module import time or emit output outside the console adapter. No import-time Click monkey-patching, no `sys.exit`/`click.echo`/`print` in lib code. A TUI/API/WebUI process must be able to `import` settings without pulling Click. *HOLDS (00052) — keep it.*\n880:- **One action, one implementation across interfaces** — CLI/TUI/API/WebUI adapters must drive the same command class / use case through the composition root and return `CommandResult`; do not reimplement business logic per interface (dot's TUI is the anti-pattern; bim's serve action registry is the exemplar). *HOLDS (00051 design → 00053/00054) — the all-interface seam design (00051) is realized: dot's git ops unified behind `DotGitService` (00053) and bim's serve/TUI converged on the shared action registry (00054). Keep it.*\n881:- **Claim/lifecycle release on any exit** — resource claims and locks release in `finally` (or catch `BaseException`), never only `except Exception`; `KeyboardInterrupt` must not leak a claim. *HOLDS (00044) — the bim doc claim/dedup pipeline releases claims and locks in `finally` (`commands/doc/shared/`); keep it.*\n882:- **`CommandResult` discipline** — commands return `CommandResult`; the CLI/adapter layer renders it (`console.report_result`) and maps exit/HTTP status. No `sys.exit`/`console.panic` inside command classes. *HOLDS in `src/tools/*/commands/**` — keep it.*\n883:- **Clean-architecture layering + tool isolation** — zettel `domain → application → infrastructure → integrations` one-directional; no cross-tool imports; no `src/lib → src/tools`. *HOLDS — keep it.*\n884:\n885:\n886:Relevant reviewed API contracts (not acceptance criteria):\n887:\n888:Keep the existing protocol-compatible signature:\n889:\n890:```python\n891:def TarArchive.run(self: TarArchive, **kwargs: object) -> Iterator[StepResult]: ...\n892:\n893:def _emit_progress(\n894:    progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None,\n895:    event: ProgressEvent,\n896:) -> None: ...\n897:\n898:def TarArchive._archive_result(\n899:    label: str, arcbase: Path, out: Path, walk: WalkResult, engine: str, *,\n900:    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n901:    progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None = None,\n902:) -> StepResult: ...\n903:\n904:def _write_archive(\n905:    out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *,\n906:    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n907:    on_bytes: Callable[[int], None] | None = None,\n908:    on_finalize: Callable[[], None] | None = None,\n909:) -> None: ...\n910:\n911:def _write_archive_system_tar(\n912:    out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *,\n913:    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n914:    on_finalize: Callable[[], None] | None = None,\n915:) -> str | None: ...\n916:```\n917:\n918:`TarArchive.run` accepts the runtime kwarg `progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None`; parse it from `kwargs`, then pass it through the existing validated-run path. Do not add it to `TarArchive.inputs`. `_emit_progress` calls a non-None callback and swallows ordinary `Exception`; it does not swallow `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `GeneratorExit` or other `BaseException`. Genuine interruption must retain the established cancellation/cleanup behavior. The runner applies the same exception policy to consumer callbacks.\n919:\n920:For a real validated run, emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"scanning\", None, None)` immediately before `_walk_tree` or `_select_items`. Invalid inputs, a missing source and preflight rejection do not enter a walk and need no fabricated activity. A bundle which becomes a skip during selection can end after scanning. For `dry_run=True`, disable the emitter before either walk and never call `progress`, including for direct capability use.\n921:\n922:After a successful walk and before writing, emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"archiving\", 0, walk.total_bytes)` for the configured Python engine, or `ProgressEvent(label, \"archiving\", None, walk.total_bytes)` for the configured system-tar engine. Set the initial throttle timestamp with `time.monotonic()` at this boundary.\n923:\n924:The Python writer calls `on_bytes(cumulative_done)` only after each successful `tar.add(..., recursive=False)`. When accounting is enabled, obtain `file_path.lstat()` immediately before adding the member; add its `st_size` only if `stat.S_ISREG(st_mode)`. Directories and all symlinks, including broken links, contribute zero and must not be followed for counting. Regular source paths that tar encodes as hardlinks still contribute their input `st_size`, consistent with `walk.total_bytes`. Keep accounting state local to this invocation; `CAPABILITIES` contains a shared singleton `TarArchive`.\n925:\n926:`_archive_result` owns the cumulative count and event throttle. Update its latest count on every writer callback; emit intermediate archiving events only when at least `0.2` monotonic seconds have elapsed since the previous archiving event. On successful archive-stream close, flush the last count as an archiving event if it differs from the last reported count, then emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"finalizing\", done, walk.total_bytes)`. The unconditional final cumulative flush is the minimal resolution of the PRD's short-archive tension: intermediate updates are limited to one per 0.2 seconds, while the required final count is never omitted and archive completion is never delayed to satisfy a UI rate limit. Scanning, the initial archiving event and phase transitions are boundary events.\n927:\n928:`on_finalize()` runs inside each writer immediately after the archive stream closes successfully and before fsync/publication: after the Python tar context exits, or after successful system-tar communication returns. It must not run for a failed stream. Existing raw-file flush, fsync, mode 0600, cancellation checks, atomic `os.replace` and `BaseException` temp cleanup remain in their current order. A finalizing event means the stream closed, not that publication succeeded; the terminal `StepResult` remains authoritative.\n929:\n930:For the configured system-tar engine, `done_bytes` is `None` for every archiving/finalizing event. A failed system attempt followed by the existing Python fallback retains this activity-only policy for the instance: pass no `on_bytes` callback to the fallback writer, but preserve finalizing and the existing fallback note. This avoids changing percentage semantics partway through a configured system-tar job. No subprocess output parsing or archive-size polling is introduced.\n931:Read-only support modules: /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/step_result.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/config.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/capabilities/__init__.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/runner.py\n932:READING BUDGET: Read tar_archive.py lines1-235 for runtime kwargs and validated/result paths, lines405-495 for writers. Other walk helpers need only signatures unless a test fails there. Ordered work: (1) safe emitter and validated phase hooks; (2) Python writer input accounting and successful-close callback, test byte cases; (3) system writer close callback/fallback activity semantics, test engine/callback cases; (4) focused changelog entry. Preserve original write durability and cancellation checks. Avoid rereading entire600-line source.\n933:\n934:Use Python patterns at /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md for Python edits. Keep modified functions under50 lines and Python files under800 lines, preserving behavior; small helpers within allowlisted source are permitted. Existing task tests are read-only. Add a focused Added CHANGELOG entry under Unreleased for this task; it is mandatory in each feature commit. Do not commit; coordinator handles commits. Native implementation route OpenAI gpt-6.1-sol/high, logical opus; independent completion review route Anthropic Opus/xhigh is supported by autocodex driver.\n935:\n936:\n937:## Files you may read and modify\n938:\n939:/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py\n940:/Users/bob/git/src/githu