s abort - parametrize OSError, TypeError, and a custom Exception subclass.\n\n## Mutant 8: System finalization swallows genuine interruption\n\n```diff\n--- baseline\n+++ mutant-8\n@@ -519,7 +519,8 @@\n             detail = stderr[-1] if stderr else f\"exit {returncode}\"\n             return f\"tar exited {returncode}: {detail}\"\n         if on_finalize is not None:\n-            on_finalize()\n+            with contextlib.suppress(BaseException):\n+                on_finalize()\n         # Match the Python engine's durability: fsync the finished temp archive\n         # before atomically swapping it into place.\n         archive_fd = os.open(str(tmp_path), os.O_RDONLY)\n```\n\nExact output:\n\n```text\n.....................................                                    [100%]\n37 passed, 18 deselected in 0.10s\nEXIT 0\nRESTORED True\n```\n\nWeak point 8: test_base_exception_is_not_swallowed_by_progress_emitter - system writer swallows propagated finalization interruption - run real system engine with finalizing callback raising KeyboardInterrupt/SystemExit; assert propagation and preserved output.\n\n\nAddress every numbered weak point. Answer each in your reply as N. strengthened: <test name> or N. in-contract: <why behavior is allowed>. Do not change tests that Devon could not break. Only test_progress.py and progress_*_cases.py split siblings writable. File is currently about665 lines: split new engine test classes to non-collected progress_engine_cases.py if needed, re-export them from test_progress.py with explicit same-name aliases so acceptance selector discovers them. Preserve prior event-record tests and tests of all existing behaviors. Run ruff format on changed test files before measuring AST under50/file800. Run only current task selector, timeout300. This is the fourth and final total Tess dispatch for task2; no unbounded retries.\n\n\nOriginal requirements (unchanged):\n\nDescription:\nEmit archive engine progress\n\nLocation: /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/capabilities/tar_archive.py\n\nReuse: Existing NamedTuple records, archive writers, completion queue, pending-result reporting and console.status as identified in reviewed design.\n\nContract (verbatim — reviewed design):\nKeep the existing protocol-compatible signature:\n\n```python\ndef TarArchive.run(self: TarArchive, **kwargs: object) -> Iterator[StepResult]: ...\n\ndef _emit_progress(\n    progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None,\n    event: ProgressEvent,\n) -> None: ...\n\ndef TarArchive._archive_result(\n    label: str, arcbase: Path, out: Path, walk: WalkResult, engine: str, *,\n    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n    progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None = None,\n) -> StepResult: ...\n\ndef _write_archive(\n    out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *,\n    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n    on_bytes: Callable[[int], None] | None = None,\n    on_finalize: Callable[[], None] | None = None,\n) -> None: ...\n\ndef _write_archive_system_tar(\n    out: Path, arcbase: Path, files: Iterable[Path], *,\n    cancel: threading.Event | None = None,\n    on_finalize: Callable[[], None] | None = None,\n) -> str | None: ...\n```\n\n`TarArchive.run` accepts the runtime kwarg `progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None`; parse it from `kwargs`, then pass it through the existing validated-run path. Do not add it to `TarArchive.inputs`. `_emit_progress` calls a non-None callback and swallows ordinary `Exception`; it does not swallow `KeyboardInterrupt`, `SystemExit`, `GeneratorExit` or other `BaseException`. Genuine interruption must retain the established cancellation/cleanup behavior. The runner applies the same exception policy to consumer callbacks.\n\nFor a real validated run, emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"scanning\", None, None)` immediately before `_walk_tree` or `_select_items`. Invalid inputs, a missing source and preflight rejection do not enter a walk and need no fabricated activity. A bundle which becomes a skip during selection can end after scanning. For `dry_run=True`, disable the emitter before either walk and never call `progress`, including for direct capability use.\n\nAfter a successful walk and before writing, emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"archiving\", 0, walk.total_bytes)` for the configured Python engine, or `ProgressEvent(label, \"archiving\", None, walk.total_bytes)` for the configured system-tar engine. Set the initial throttle timestamp with `time.monotonic()` at this boundary.\n\nThe Python writer calls `on_bytes(cumulative_done)` only after each successful `tar.add(..., recursive=False)`. When accounting is enabled, obtain `file_path.lstat()` immediately before adding the member; add its `st_size` only if `stat.S_ISREG(st_mode)`. Directories and all symlinks, including broken links, contribute zero and must not be followed for counting. Regular source paths that tar encodes as hardlinks still contribute their input `st_size`, consistent with `walk.total_bytes`. Keep accounting state local to this invocation; `CAPABILITIES` contains a shared singleton `TarArchive`.\n\n`_archive_result` owns the cumulative count and event throttle. Update its latest count on every writer callback; emit intermediate archiving events only when at least `0.2` monotonic seconds have elapsed since the previous archiving event. On successful archive-stream close, flush the last count as an archiving event if it differs from the last reported count, then emit `ProgressEvent(label, \"finalizing\", done, walk.total_bytes)`. The unconditional final cumulative flush is the minimal resolution of the PRD's short-archive tension: intermediate updates are limited to one per 0.2 seconds, while the required final count is never omitted and archive completion is never delayed to satisfy a UI rate limit. Scanning, the initial archiving event and phase transitions are boundary events.\n\n`on_finalize()` runs inside each writer immediately after the archive stream closes successfully and before fsync/publication: after the Python tar context exits, or after successful system-tar communication returns. It must not run for a failed stream. Existing raw-file flush, fsync, mode 0600, cancellation checks, atomic `os.replace` and `BaseException` temp cleanup remain in their current order. A finalizing event means the stream closed, not that publication succeeded; the terminal `StepResult` remains authoritative.\n\nFor the configured system-tar engine, `done_bytes` is `None` for every archiving/finalizing event. A failed system attempt followed by the existing Python fallback retains this activity-only policy for the instance: pass no `on_bytes` callback to the fallback writer, but preserve finalizing and the existing fallback note. This avoids changing percentage semantics partway through a configured system-tar job. No subprocess output parsing or archive-size polling is introduced.\n\nDetails:\n- Add focused tests to /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py. Preserve earlier tasks' tests. Use names matching this acceptance selector. If splitting for 800-line limit, use non-collected sibling progress_*_cases.py and import test classes into test_progress.py.\n- Test failure, empty/unknown values and cancellation behavior where applicable; preserve previous interfaces.\n\nAcceptance criteria:\n- uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py -k \"engine or input_bytes or swallowed\" -q passes.\n\nVerify: uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py -k \"engine or input_bytes or swallowed\" -q\n\nAdditional PRD acceptance criteria (verbatim):\n- `test_percent_uses_input_bytes_not_output_size` passes: with an incompressible and a highly compressible fixture, the reported done bytes equal the summed sizes of the files added.\n\n\nAcceptance Criteria:\n\n- uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py -k \"engine or input_bytes or swallowed\" -q passes.\n\nVerify: uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py -k \"engine or input_bytes or swallowed\" -q\n\nAdditional PRD acceptance criteria (verbatim):\n- `test_percent_uses_input_bytes_not_output_size` passes: with an incompressible and a highly compressible fixture, the reported done bytes equal the summed sizes of the files added.\n\n\nRewrite the tests addressing each issue above. Keep every test that is already\nsound — this is a targeted fix, not a rewrite from scratch.\n\nThe original rules still apply. In particular:\n\n1. Write tests from requirements only. You are a USER of this API, not its builder.\n2. Each test name describes a behavior, not an implementation detail.\n3. Assert on outputs, return values, side effects, and errors. Never assert on mock existence.\n4. For EACH test, ask yourself: \"What wrong implementation would still pass this?\" If easy to answer, add constraints.\n5. SIMPLICITY FIRST: test only the behavior in the acceptance criteria. Strengthening a weak test does not mean adding tests nobody asked for.\n6. NEVER INVENT A CONTRACT: if the requirements name a data structure but do not pin its exact field names, enum values, or types, STOP and report it as a blocker rather than inventing a plausible schema.\n7. SURGICAL: only create/modify test files. Match the existing test file's style.\n8. SIZE LIMITS: keep every test function under 50 lines and every test file under 800 lines; a style-gate line above naming a file or function over a limit is an issue to fix here, by splitting into sibling files named after the test module.\n\nRead only the files listed above. If a file or symbol you need is not listed, stop and report it as a blocker — do not run broad `rg` sweeps to discover scope.\n\nAbort and report if you read more than 100K of total input. Return the partial result and an abort_reason: context_overrun field.\n\nRead every file before your first Edit to it. Never call bash `head`, `tail`, `cat`, `grep`, or `find` - a hook blocks them. Use the Read tool (offset/limit), `rg`, or `rg --files` instead. Never combine an inspection (read, list, search, diff) with a test, lint or build invocation in one Bash call - run them as two calls, because a combined command has two ways to hang and one exit code. Pass an explicit `timeout` on every Bash call: 60000 ms for an inspection, 300000 ms for a lint run or a narrow test run, 600000 ms for a full suite or a full build.\n\nRun only the test files this task names (the tests you wrote, or the\nfailing tests in your prompt), with `-q --tb=line`. Never run a whole test\ndirectory or `dev/bin/release-checks`: the orchestrator runs the full suite\nonce, after every task. Read the pass count and exit code from that one\nrun; never re-run a suite to recover a number.\n\nEnd your report with `ASSUMPTIONS:` - one line per assumption you made where the task, tests, or listed files were silent (guessed interface, data shape, resolved ambiguity, unstated behavior). Write `ASSUMPTIONS: none` if you made none.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2250}},"started_at_ms":1793566406258,"completed_at_ms":1793566406258}}
