ce. When `on_progress is None` or `dry_run=True`, pass `progress=None` instead. No callbacks are invoked by `plan()`. Suppress both callbacks for dry runs.\n\nWhile work remains pending, drain currently queued progress with `get_nowait()` before waiting for a completion, then use `completed.get(timeout=0.2)`. Catch `queue.Empty` as a normal wake-up, drain progress again, and invoke the optional tick on the iteration thread. Drain on completion notification as well. Drain before every terminal result is yielded, including ready preflight rows and cancellation outcomes, and once after joined workers finish during normal cancellation. These drains unblock bounded-queue producers even when no future has completed. Enqueuing is synchronous inside the capability, before its future completes, so a completed uncancelled instance's entire queued progress prefix is available before its result. Preserve FIFO event order per label. Cross-label event order is queue insertion order and need not equal configured launch order. After cancellation, no new progress enqueue is required; drain every event already queued before terminal cancellation results.\n\nTicks run on the thread advancing `Runner.run`, only while the generator is actively executing. The wait loop offers a tick at least every 0.2 seconds during an otherwise idle wait, subject to scheduler/callback execution time; no guarantee applies while the consumer holds a yielded result or the existing blocking cancellation join runs. A callback must be short and nonblocking. Catch ordinary exceptions from each `on_progress`/`on_tick` invocation separately so one broken observer cannot turn any archive into a failure. Preserve calling-thread `KeyboardInterrupt` handling, request cancellation and drain the same run.\n\nOn generator abandonment, set cancellation before joining writers, so producers blocked on the bounded queue observe cancellation after at most their current 0.2-second put timeout and return. Do not invoke user callbacks from the abandonment-only `finally` path. Normal cancellation likewise sets cancellation before shutdown/join, then drains the joined queue before yielding results. Release per-run queue references on exit. A subsequent run uses clean queues and cannot receive earlier events. Do not refactor outcomes, submission boundaries, lost-notification recovery or cancellation ordering as part of this feature.\nRead-only support modules: /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/step_result.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/config.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/capabilities/__init__.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/runner.py\nExisting regression seam: test_runner_interrupt_boundaries.py and test_parallel_runner.py monkeypatch backup.runner.queue.Queue to inject completion-wait interruptions. Preserve that completion queue seam when adding a separate bounded progress queue; use separate construction ownership if necessary. Do not edit those tests. Existing _run_instance callers may omit any new private optional progress argument. Read Runner._execute near81 and _run_instance near189 first. Keep timed waits, bounded enqueue cancellation and cleanup semantics explicit; isolate observer exceptions. New mandatory callback/context arguments may need narrowly justified PLR0913 noqa rather than changing public signature contracts.\n\nUse Python patterns at /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md for Python edits. Keep modified functions under50 lines and Python files under800 lines, preserving behavior; small helpers within allowlisted source are permitted. Existing task tests are read-only. Add a focused Added CHANGELOG entry under Unreleased for this task; it is mandatory in each feature commit. Do not commit; coordinator handles commits. Native implementation route OpenAI gpt-6.1-sol/high, logical opus; independent completion review route Anthropic Opus/xhigh is supported by autocodex driver.\n\n\n## Files you may read and modify\n\n/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/runner.py\n/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/CHANGELOG.md\n\nRead only the files listed above. If a file or symbol you need is not\nlisted, stop and report it as a blocker — do not run broad `rg` sweeps to\ndiscover scope.\n\n\n\n### Code quality rules (mandatory)\n\nFollow these four rules. They override any instinct to \"improve\" the code\nbeyond what the failing tests require.\n\n**1. Think before coding.** Re-read the task and the failing tests. If scope,\ndata shape, target file, or success criteria are ambiguous - more than one\nreasonable implementation would pass the tests differently - STOP and report\nthe ambiguity as a blocker. Do not guess and proceed.\n\n**2. Simplicity.** Write the simplest code that makes the failing tests pass.\n\n- No abstractions, helpers, or interfaces built for a single caller.\n- No error handling, fallbacks, or validation for inputs the tests never\n  exercise. Validate at real system boundaries only.\n- No features, options, or configuration the task did not ask for.\n- If your draft runs long and the same behavior fits in far fewer lines,\n  rewrite it before returning.\n\n**3. Surgical changes.** Touch only what the task requires.\n\n- Do not refactor, reformat, or \"improve\" code outside the change.\n- Match the surrounding style even if you would write it differently.\n- Remove imports, variables, and functions that YOUR change orphaned. Do not\n  delete pre-existing dead code - mention it instead.\n- Every changed line must trace to the task or a failing test.\n\n**4. Goal-driven.** The failing tests are the spec. Make them pass - do not\nmodify them, do not weaken them, do not add tests. If a test looks wrong,\nreport it as a blocker rather than changing it.\n\nAbort and report if you read more than 100K of total input. Return the\npartial result and an abort_reason: context_overrun field.\n\nRead every file before your first Edit to it. Never call bash `head`,\n`tail`, `cat`, `grep`, or `find` - a hook blocks them. Use the Read tool\n(offset/limit), `rg`, or `rg --files` instead. Never combine an inspection\n(read, list, search, diff) with a test, lint or build invocation in one Bash\ncall - run them as two calls, because a combined command has two ways to hang\nand one exit code. Pass an explicit `timeout` on every Bash call: 60000 ms for\nan inspection, 300000 ms for a lint run or a narrow test run, 600000 ms for a\nfull suite or a full build.\n\nRun only the test files this task names (the tests you wrote, or the\nfailing tests in your prompt), with `-q --tb=line`. Never run a whole test\ndirectory or `dev/bin/release-checks`: the orchestrator runs the full suite\nonce, after every task. Read the pass count and exit code from that one\nrun; never re-run a suite to recover a number.\n\nEnd your report with `ASSUMPTIONS:` - one line per assumption you made\nwhere the task, tests, or listed files were silent (guessed interface, data\nshape, resolved ambiguity, unstated behavior). Write `ASSUMPTIONS: none` if\nyou made none.\n\nAlso end your report with `FILES_TOUCHED:` - one line per file you created\nor modified, path relative to the repo root. Write `FILES_TOUCHED: none` if\nyou changed no files.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1667}},"started_at_ms":1793568109221,"completed_at_ms":1793568109221}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:21:49.224Z","ordinal":41,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a198fd-33c0-7473-add8-0b52249fd26f","turn_id":"01a198fd-33f1-70c2-89d9-ea1eb12b98ca","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-3c3bc174-8139-4919-8dff-ddad89a3df70","process_id":"71307","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","sed -n '1,240p' /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md","name":"SKILL.md","path":"/Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"---\nname: python-patterns\ndescription: Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Python code. Covers idioms, naming, traps, toolchain, and security. Triggers on Python file edits, \"pythonic\", \"PEP 8\", \"python best practices\", \"python idioms\", \"bandit\".\ncompatibility: \"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\"\n---\n\n# Python Patterns\n\nPick a side. These are the rulings, not a tutorial.\n\n## Idioms that earn their place\n\n| Rule | Why |\n|------|-----|\n| `X \\| Y`, not `Union[X, Y]` | 3.10+ runtime syntax; clearer. On 3.9 it parses only under `from __future__ import annotations` and still breaks `get_type_hints`/`isinstance` — keep `Optional`/`Union` if you support 3.9. |\n| `Protocol`, not `ABC`, for duck typing | Structural typing; no inheritance you don't control. |\n| `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for immutable records, plain `dataclass` only when you must mutate, `NamedTuple` for lightweight immutable rows | Pydantic only when you need validation. |\n| `raise New(...) from e` | Marks the explicit cause. A bare `raise New()` still chains implicitly (\"During handling...\"); use `from None` to suppress. |\n| `@functools.wraps` on every decorator | Without it the wrapper loses `__name__`, `__doc__`, signature. |\n| `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` | One object, no string surgery. |\n\n## Naming (PEP 8)\n\n`snake_case` for modules, functions, variables. `CapWords` for classes. `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for constants. Leading `_` for internal names.\n\n**Never name a `.py` file in kebab-case.** `my-script.py` is not importable (`import my-script` is a `SyntaxError`). Kebab-case belongs only in the `pyproject.toml` distribution name and the CLI command, both of which map to a `snake_case` module.\n\n## Traps\n\n```python\n# Mutable default argument — shared across calls\ndef bad(items=[]): ...\ndef good(items=None):\n    items = [] if items is None else items\n\n# Bare except — swallows SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt\ntry: ...\nexcept Exception as e: ...        # name the exception\n\n# __slots__ kills __dict__ and dynamic attributes — use only on fixed,\n# memory-sensitive classes you won't extend.\n\n# __double_leading is name-mangled to _Class__double_leading.\n# Surprises you in subclasses, pickling, and monkey-patching.\n\nif value is None: ...             # never == None\n```\n\nAvoid `from module import *`. Use `isinstance`, never `type(x) ==`.\n\n## Toolchain\n\n**black** to format, **isort** for imports, **ruff** to lint. Type annotations on every function signature — no exceptions for \"obvious\" returns.\n\n## Resources and laziness\n\nContext managers (`with`) for anything holding a resource. Generators for lazy iteration over anything you would otherwise materialize into a list.\n\n## Security\n\n- Read secrets with `os.environ[\"API_KEY\"]`, never `.get()` — a missing secret must raise `KeyError` at startup, not surface as `None` at request time.\n- `python-dotenv` loads a local `.env` in development only. Never commit one.\n- `bandit -r src/` for static security analysis.\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":1750}},"started_at_ms":1793568109224,"completed_at_ms":1793568109224}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:21:49.225Z","ordinal":42,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a198fd-33c0-7473-add8-0b52249fd26f","turn_id":"01a198fd-33f1-70c2-89d9-ea1eb12b98ca","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-233d5466-35ca-436a-923a-d1b15a11b6b3","process_id":"3496","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","sed -n '1,100p' CHANGELOG.md"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"read","cmd":"sed -n '1,100p' CHANGELOG.md","name":"CHANGELOG.md","path":"CHANGELOG.md"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"# Changelog\n\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n\n## [Unreleased]\n\n### Added\n\n- **backup**: archive engines report scanning, archiving and finalizing progress; Python archives include throttled input byte counts, and consumer errors do not stop backups.\n- **backup**: immutable `ProgressEvent` records carry an instance label, phase and optional input byte counts.\n- **backup**: `--jobs` controls concurrent archive instances (default three); cancellation stops new work, joins workers, stops tar and cleans temporary files while preserving completed results and backups; interruption exits with status 130.\n- **backup**: `--plan` previews metadata-only run/skip/fail decisions, reasons and existing-output replacement markers; selected outputs share one timestamp and colliding paths are rejected before plan, run or dry-run execution.\n- **backup**: optional `items` bundle literal source-relative files and directories into one archive, preserving empty directories and symlinks while applying inherited exclusions and `.bkpignore` rules.\n- **backup**: per-instance `inherit_excludes`, `exclude_remove` and `exclude_add` control inherited exclusions for archives and `--show-excludes`.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **backup**: missing sources and bundles with no surviving members now warn and skip without touching previous archives; each run reports archived or previewed, skipped, and failed step counts. Existing non-directory sources and unreadable selected files still fail.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **backup**: interruption during submission or result collection preserves every instance's outcome and finishes cleanup without waiting for a lost completion notification.\n- **backup**: a source or output metadata error while planning one instance now fails only that instance; the others still run, and duplicate outputs are still rejected.\n- **backup**: `tar-archive` plans treat an empty `engine` as the default Python engine and report an empty `source` or `out` as \"no source configured\" or \"no out path configured\", the same as a run, instead of using the working directory; two instances with an empty `out` no longer report a false output collision.\n- **sysup**: a `timeout` on a captured multi-command shell step now stops the whole step; before, only bash died and the command it was waiting on kept running. Such a step runs in its own process group, so Ctrl-C is forwarded to it, a terminal prompt it raises stops it until the timeout, and a command it runs as root through `sudo` is still out of reach and survives. Interactive steps and steps without a timeout are unchanged\n\n## [0.16.0] - 2026-10-29\n\n### Added\n\n- **sysup**: a step in a run entry, and a refresh command, may be written as a plain shell line such as `HOMEBREW_NO_UPGRADE_AUTO_UPDATES_CASKS=1 brew upgrade --quiet`, run by `bash -eo pipefail -c`, so variables, pipes, `&&` and `VAR=1 cmd` prefixes work as typed and a failing command anywhere in a pipe fails the step; an argv array still runs without a shell, and either may be an `{argv, env}` object whose `env` is merged over the parent environment for that step only (`~` expanded). An empty step is rejected when the config loads instead of failing mid-run\n\n### Changed\n\n- **sysup**: the bundled entries are written as shell lines; `brew` upgrades with `HOMEBREW_NO_UPGRADE_AUTO_UPDATES_CASKS=1` and `--quiet`, leaving self-updating casks to their own updater; sudo priming triggers on any step that invokes `sudo`, not only one whose argv starts with it\n\n## [0.15.3] - 2026-10-28\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **sysup**: the bundled `brew upgrade` passes `--no-ask`, so Homebrew no longer stops the run with a \"Do you want to proceed with the upgrade?\" prompt now that the step is interactive\n- **sysup**: the pipx-python step also walks `config.local.toml` beside the mise config, so pipx tools kept in the machine-local file follow the global python minor bump too\n\n## [0.15.2] - 2026-10-28\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **sysup**: the bundled `brew` step runs interactively, so the sudo password prompt Homebrew raises for casks is visible instead of hiding behind the spinner and freezing the run\n\n## [0.15.1] - 2026-10-27\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **sysup**: `uv-tool-python` now moves editable and otherwise customized uv tool installs with `uv tool upgrade --python`, so a host with an editable tool no longer ends every run red; only malformed receipts are skipped; tools already on the global minor are left alone, and a failed move restores the receipt uv deleted before rolling back\n\n## [0.15.0] - 2026-10-25\n\n### Added\n\n- **sysup**: `uv-tool-python` runs before uv upgrades on macOS, moving supported plain freestanding uv tools with older numeric Python pins or no pin to the global mise Python minor. Equal and newer pins stay unchanged. Failed moves attempt rollback and report recovery failures; customized or malformed receipts are visibly skipped untouched and may still fail during later uv upgrades.\n- **dot**: `dot localize --shared <path>` opts a tracked file into a git clean/smudge filter instead of moving it: the file stays where it is, git stores the reserved `@HOME@` token in place of this host's home path, and each host's work tree gets its own home back on checkout. Combining `--shared` with `--to` is refused before any change. The attribute line is staged before the file is renormalized, so a failed share never leaves a tokenized file in the index without its attribute. The filter itself is hidden plumbing git calls (`dot filter clean|smudge <path>`): a pure byte transform that preserves line endings, BOM and non-UTF-8 content, and refuses any file that already contains `@HOME@`.\n- **sysup**: a `sudo: true` flag on an entry (or any step starting with `sudo`) primes a cached sudo ticket before the run, refreshed in the background past sudo's 5-minute timeout; press Ctrl-D at the password prompt to skip priming (later steps then prompt for themselves as before), or Ctrl-C to abort the run entirely. A missing `sudo` binary or a declined prompt is not an error. Each run writes a per-run log under `$XDG_STATE_HOME/sysup/logs` (falling back to `~/.local/state/sysup/logs`), keeping the newest 10 files; a same-second filename collision gets a `-1`, `-2`, ... suffix. `sysup` prints the log path after every run that reaches one, clean, amber or red. A captured step's stdout/stderr lines starting with `error:` (case-insensitive) are flagged even on a zero exit code; up to 5 are printed inline, any more are summarized as `+N more, see log`. One entry producing any such lines reports a single `warned` result (amber, not red) instead of the usual success.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **sysup**: a `run:` entry now yields exactly one final `StepResult` for its whole `steps` list (success, failure, or `warned`), not one per step\n- **sysup**: a run-log write failure now stops the run instead of continuing silently\n\n## [0.14.2] - 2026-10-17\n\n### Added\n\n- **sysup**: an `expiry:` registry of credentials with a hard expiry, checked before every ordinary run and fed into the attention summary, state, push and exit code: built-in `gh-token-expiry` (expiration header plus a private-repository grant probe; a disabled `gh-agent` template ships, enable it locally with your probe) and `x509-expiry` (PEM file or TLS endpoint, needs the new `sysup-cert` extra); commands can declare `needs: [item]` and are skipped with a `sysup refresh <item>` hint while the item is expired or unknown; `sysup refresh <item>` walks through renewal on a terminal, passes a pasted secret only on the stdin of the command that declares `stdin: secret`, and re-checks the item\n- **dot**: `dot setup [URL]` brings up dotfiles on a fresh host: bare-clones the repo (URL argument or `BUVIS_DOT_REMOTE_URL`) into `<dotfiles_root>/.buvis`, hides untracked files, sets the upstream, checks out (refusing, never forcing, when existing files conflict, ignored ones included) and materializes host links and hooks. Every dot command now sets git `core.hooksPath` to `<dotfiles_root>/.config/buvis/hooks` when unset, never changing a set value, and `dot status` warns when the hooks dir or its `pre-commit` hook is missing.\n- **sysup**: an end-of-run summary of everything unresolved, persisted in $XDG_STATE_HOME/sysup/attention.json until the failing command completes cleanly, pushed through ntfy (config `ntfy:` url/topic/credentials_file, env NTFY_URL/NTFY_TOPIC) once per run, and `sysup status` to read it back without running anything\n- **dot**: `dot localize <target> [--to <source>]` command converts a tracked file to a host-specific link in one step: untrack, move to source location, create symlink, append to manifest, stage the manifest. `dot pull` now detects incoming deletions of host-link targets and rescues local bytes before the merge — moves become automatic, and collisions (both target and source exist with different content) prompt for resolution: keep-source (backup target), keep-target (backup old source), or cancel. The TUI (key `P`) shows a modal for each collision. Warning: raw `git pull` bypasses rescue — always use `dot pull` to protect host-specific files.\n- **dot**: host-specific config links: a tracked `~/.config/buvis/host-links` manifest of `<target> => <source>` lines (both `$HOME`-relative); bad lines are reported as `host-links:<line>: <reason>` and skipped. New `dot materialize [--hook]` makes each target a link to its host-local source (a junction for directories on Windows); real files, wrong links, dangling links and missing sources are reported, never deleted or overwritten. Outside `--hook` it also installs missing `post-checkout`, `post-merge` and `post-rewrite` hooks into git `core.hooksPath` so links refresh after git operations; existing hook files are never changed. `dot status` lists link problems; the TUI status bar shows their count and `L` relinks.\n\n### Changed\n\n- **sysup**: exits 1 when a step failed during the run (was 0); \"expiring\" outcomes and entries retained from earlier runs keep exit 0\n- **dot**: `dot pull` no longer fails with \"would be overwritten\" when an uncommitted edit touches a file the incoming commits change: it saves those edits to a `git apply`-able divergence patch in `~/.local/share/dot/divergence/`, resets the files to `HEAD` and lets upstream win. A file with both staged and unstaged changes stops the pull untouched. `dot status` and the TUI status bar warn while a patch is pending.\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **dot**: pull no longer overwrites a locally edited git-secret file whose upstream copy changed; the edit is saved encrypted in the divergence patch\n- **dot**: `dot add <file>` tracks a file the repository has never seen instead of answering `No changes.`; a path relative to the current directory resolves there before the dotfiles root; an ignored file is refused with the matching rule and the `git add -f` escape; paths are shell-quoted and matched literally, so `file[1].txt` no longer also stages `file1.txt`; a path that exists nowhere is an error instead of a fall-through to the whole-tree picker; afterwards the command prints what is currently staged for the path, or warns when nothing is.\n- **sysup**: the mise step updates mise itself without a confirmation prompt, which sysup could never answer, so it no longer fails with \"self-update cancelled\".\n- **pybase**: config merge no longer mutates an earlier layer's nested dict when a later layer overrides the same key.\n- **pybase**: auto-update asks the installer's index (mise, uv, pip) what it can install, not PyPI, so a lagging mirror or release-age gate no longer costs a useless upgrade and restart on every run. An upgrade that installs nothing is logged as an error and skipped for that version for 24 hours. `--update` says when PyPI is ahead of the installer's index and fails when the upgrade did not apply.\n\n## [0.14.1] - 2026-10-12\n\n### Fixed\n\n- **pybase**: stop every CLI hanging in an endless silent restart loop when the installer reports success without installing (e.g. mise hides releases younger than its `minimum_release_age`); the auto-update now runs at most once per invocation.\n\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":875}},"started_at_ms":1793568109225,"completed_at_ms":1793568109225}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:21:49.227Z","ordinal":43,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a198fd-72ab-7010-a98a-75c8926cba7c","call_id":"call_5f6e80c6f43e4d2c8ab41d13d852b803","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"c30740\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000001667,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2138,\"output\":\"```python\\ndef Runner.__init__(\\n    self: Runner, cfg: BackupConfig, *, dry_run: bool = False, jobs: int = 3,\\n    on_progress: Callable[[ProgressEvent], None] | None = None,\\n    on_tick: Callable[[], None] | None = None,\\n) -> None: ...\\n```\\n\\nKeep `Runner.run`, `request_cancel`, `plan`, `interrupted` and `cancelled_names` contracts unchanged. `on_tick` is an optional, backward-compatible refinement of the PRD's wake-up requirement: a wake-up with no event needs a distinct hook to recompute elapsed text. Never replay an old `ProgressEvent`, synthesize a heartbeat phase, yield non-results or create a CLI timer thread to implement a tick.\\n\\nEach `run()` creates a fresh `queue.Queue[ProgressEvent](maxsize=256)` for progress, separate from the existing completed-future queue. The progress queue holds at most 256 events. Workers receive only the enqueue callable as `progress`; they never receive `on_progress`, `on_tick` or a console object. The enqueue callable checks the run's cancellation event, then calls `put(event, timeout=0.2)`; on `queue.Full`, retry while cancellation remains unset. If cancellation becomes set, return without enqueueing or raising. A queue timeout is backpressure, never an archive failure. No event is silently dropped for an uncancelled successful instance. When `on_progress is None` or `dry_run=True`, pass `progress=None` instead. No callbacks are invoked by `plan()`. Suppress both callbacks for dry runs.\\n\\nWhile work remains pending, drain currently queued progress with `get_nowait()` before waiting for a completion, then use `completed.get(timeout=0.2)`. Catch `queue.Empty` as a normal wake-up, drain progress again, and invoke the optional tick on the iteration thread. Drain on completion notification as well. Drain before every terminal result is yielded, including ready preflight rows and cancellation outcomes, and once after joined workers finish during normal cancellation. These drains unblock bounded-queue producers even when no future has completed. Enqueuing is synchronous inside the capability, before its future completes, so a completed uncancelled instance's entire queued progress prefix is available before its result. Preserve FIFO event order per label. Cross-label event order is queue insertion order and need not equal configured launch order. After cancellation, no new progress enqueue is required; drain every event already queued before terminal cancellation results.\\n\\nTicks run on the thread advancing `Runner.run`, only while the generator is actively executing. The wait loop offers a tick at least every 0.2 seconds during an otherwise idle wait, subject to scheduler/callback execution time; no guarantee applies while the consumer holds a yielded result or the existing blocking cancellation join runs. A callback must be short and nonblocking. Catch ordinary exceptions from each `on_progress`/`on_tick` invocation separately so one broken observer cannot turn any archive into a failure. Preserve calling-thread `KeyboardInterrupt` handling, request cancellation and drain the same run.\\n\\nOn generator abandonment, set cancellation before joining writers, so producers blocked on the bounded queue observe cancellation after at most their current 0.2-second put timeout and return. Do not invoke user callbacks from the abandonment-only `finally` path. Normal cancellation likewise sets cancellation before shutdown/join, then drains the joined queue before yielding results. Release per-run queue references on exit. A subsequent run uses clean queues and cannot receive earlier events. Do not refactor outcomes, submission boundaries, lost-notification recovery or cancellation ordering as part of this feature.\\nRead-only support modules: /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/step_result.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/config.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/capabilities/__init__.py, /Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/runner.py\\nExisting regression seam: test_runner_interrupt_boundaries.py and test_parallel_runner.py monkeypatch backup.runner.queue.Queue to inject completion-wait interruptions. Preserve that completion queue seam when adding a separate bounded progress queue; use separate construction ownership if necessary. Do not edit those tests. Existing _run_instance callers may omit any new private optional progress argument. Read Runner._execute near81 and _run_instance near189 first. Keep timed waits, bounded enqueue cancellation and cleanup semantics explicit; isolate observer exceptions. New mandatory callback/context arguments may need narrowly justified PLR0913 noqa rather than changing public signature contracts.\\n\\nUse Python patterns at /Users/bob/.agents/skills/python-patterns/SKILL.md for Python edits. Keep modified functions under50 lines and Python files under800 lines, preserving behavior; small helpers within allowlisted source are permitted. Existing task tests are read-only. Add a focused Added CHANGELOG entry under Unreleased for this task; it is mandatory in each feature commit. Do not commit; coordinator handles commits. Native implementation route OpenAI gpt-6.1-sol/high, logical opus; independent completion review route Anthropic Opus/xhigh is supported by autocodex driver.\\n\\n\\n## Files you may read and modify\\n\\n/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/src/tools/backup/runner.py\\n/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems/CHANGELOG.md\\n\\nRead only the files listed above. If a file or symbol you need is not\\nlisted, stop and report it as a blocker — do not run broad `rg` sweeps to\\ndiscover scope.\\n\\n\\n\\n### Code quality rules (mandatory)\\n\\nFollow these four rules. They override any instinct to \\\"improve\\\" the code\\nbeyond what the failing tests require.\\n\\n**1. Think before coding.** Re-read the task and the failing tests. If scope,\\ndata shape, target file, or success criteria are ambiguous - more than one\\nreasonable implementation would pass the tests differently - STOP and report\\nthe ambiguity as a blocker. Do not guess and proceed.\\n\\n**2. Simplicity.** Write the simplest code that makes the failing tests pass.\\n\\n- No abstractions, helpers, or interfaces built for a single caller.\\n- No error handling, fallbacks, or validation for inputs the tests never\\n  exercise. Validate at real system boundaries only.\\n- No features, options, or configuration the task did not ask for.\\n- If your draft runs long and the same behavior fits in far fewer lines,\\n  rewrite it before returning.\\n\\n**3. Surgical changes.** Touch only what the task requires.\\n\\n- Do not refactor, reformat, or \\\"improve\\\" code outside the change.\\n- Match the surrounding style even if you would write it differently.\\n- Remove imports, variables, and functions that YOUR change orphaned. Do not\\n  delete pre-existing dead code - mention it instead.\\n- Every changed line must trace to the task or a failing test.\\n\\n**4. Goal-driven.** The failing tests are the spec. Make them pass - do not\\nmodify them, do not weaken them, do not add tests. If a test looks wrong,\\nreport it as a blocker rather than changing it.\\n\\nAbort and report if you read more than 100K of total input. Return the\\npartial result and an abort_reason: context_overrun field.\\n\\nRead every file before your first Edit to it. Never call bash `head`,\\n`tail`, `cat`, `grep`, or `find` - a hook blocks them. Use the Read tool\\n(offset/limit), `rg`, or `rg --files` instead. Never combine an inspection\\n(read, list, search, diff) with a test, lint or build invocation in one Bash\\ncall - run them as two calls, because a combined command has two ways to hang\\nand one exit code. Pass an explicit `timeout` on every Bash call: 60000 ms for\\nan inspection, 300000 ms for a lint run or a narrow test run, 600000 ms for a\\nfull suite or a full build.\\n\\nRun only the test files this task names (the tests you wrote, or the\\nfailing tests in your prompt), with `-q --tb=line`. Never run a whole test\\ndirectory or `dev/bin/release-checks`: the orchestrator runs the full suite\\nonce, after every task. Read the pass count and exit code from that one\\nrun; never re-run a suite to recover a number.\\n\\nEnd your report with `ASSUMPTIONS:` - one line per assumption you made\\nwhere the task, tests, or listed files were silent (guessed interface, data\\nshape, resolved ambiguity, unstated behavior). Write `ASSUMPTIONS: none` if\\nyou made none.\\n\\nAlso end your report with `FILES_TOUCHED:` - one line per file you created\\nor modified, path relative to the repo root. Write `FILES_TOUCHED: none` if\\nyou changed no files.\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"120b55\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000002459,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":2283,\"output\":\"from __future__ import annotations\\n\\nimport os\\nimport queue\\nimport threading\\nfrom collections import deque\\nfrom concurrent.futures import Future, ThreadPoolExecutor\\nfrom datetime import datetime\\nfrom typing import TYPE_CHECKING\\n\\nfrom buvis.pybase.result import FatalError\\n\\nfrom backup.capabilities import CAPABILITIES\\nfrom backup.capabilities.tar_archive import ArchiveCancelled\\nfrom backup.config import effective_excludes\\nfrom backup.step_result import PlanEntry, StepResult\\n\\nif TYPE_CHECKING:\\n    from collections.abc import Generator, Iterator\\n\\n    from backup.config import BackupConfig, BackupInstance\\n\\n__all__ = [\\\"Runner\\\"]\\n\\n\\nclass Runner:\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"Execute selected backup instances, yielding a :class:`StepResult` per step.\\n\\n    Each instance invokes its registered capability with the instance's ``with:``\\n    inputs plus the runtime context the capability needs: the resolved instance\\n    ``excludes`` list, the instance ``label``, and the ``dry_run`` flag. Under\\n    ``dry_run`` the capability itself previews (walks + reports) without writing.\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\\n    def __init__(self: Runner, cfg: BackupConfig, *, dry_run: bool = False, jobs: int = 3) -> None:\\n        if jobs < 1:\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"jobs must be at least 1\\\")\\n        self._cfg = cfg\\n        self._dry_run = dry_run\\n        self._jobs = jobs\\n        self._cancel: threading.Event | None = None\\n        self._cancelled: set[str] = set()\\n        self._cancelled_lock = threading.Lock()\\n        self.interrupted = False\\n        self.cancelled_names: tuple[str, ...] = ()\\n\\n    def request_cancel(self: Runner) -> None:\\n        \\\"\\\"\\\"Signal cancellation without waiting for workers or rendering output.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n        self.interrupted = True\\n        if self._cancel is not None:\\n            self._cancel.set()\\n\\n    def run(self: Runner, selected: list[tuple[str, BackupInstance]]) -> Generator[StepResult, None, None]:\\n        self.interrupted = False\\n        self.cancelled_names = ()\\n        self._cancelled.clear()\\n        cancel = threading.Event()\\n        self._cancel = cancel\\n        now = datetime.now()\\n        ordered = sorted(selected, key=lambda item: (item[1].order, item[0]))\\n        remaining = deque(ordered)\\n        ready: deque[StepResult] = deque()\\n        try:\\n            try:\\n                entries = self._plan(selected, now)\\n                failures = {\\n                    name: entry.reason\\n                    for (name, _), entry in zip(selected, entries, strict=True)\\n                    if entry.status == \\\"fail\\\"\\n                }\\n                ready.extend(StepResult(name, success=False, message=reason) for name, reason in failures.items())\\n                remaining = deque((name, instance) for name, instance in ordered if name not in failures)\\n            except KeyboardInterrupt:\\n                self.request_cancel()\\n\\n            yield from self._execute(remaining, ready, now, cancel)\\n        finally:\\n            self.cancelled_names = tuple(name for name, _ in ordered if name in self._cancelled)\\n            self._cancel = None\\n\\n    def _execute(\\n        self: Runner,\\n        remaining: deque[tuple[str, BackupInstance]],\\n        ready: deque[StepResult],\\n        now: datetime,\\n        cancel: threading.Event,\\n    ) -> Iterator[StepResult]:\\n        completed: queue.Queue[Future[list[StepResult]]] = queue.Queue()\\n        pending: dict[Future[list[StepResult]], str] = {}\\n        outcomes: dict[str, list[StepResult]] = {}\\n        executor: ThreadPoolExecutor | None = None\\n        finished = False\\n        try:\\n            while ready or remaining or pending or outcomes:\\n                try:\\n                    if cancel.is_set():\\n                        if executor is not None:\\n                            self._shutdown(executor)\\n                            executor = None\\n                        names = dict.fromkeys((*pending.values(), *(name for name, _ in remaining)))\\n                        ready.extend(row for rows in outcomes.values() for row in rows)\\n                        ready.extend(self._cancelled_result(name) for name in names if name not in outcomes)\\n                        remaining.clear()\\n                        pending.clear()\\n                        outcomes.clear()\\n                    if ready:\\n                        yield ready.popleft()\\n                        continue\\n\\n                    while remaining and len(pending) < self._jobs and not cancel.is_set():\\n                        if executor is None:\\n                            executor = ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=self._jobs)\\n                        name, instance = remaining[0]\\n                        future = executor.submit(self._run_instance, name, instance, now, cancel, outcomes)\\n                        pending[future] = name\\n                        remaining.popleft()\\n                        future.add_done_callback(completed.put)\\n                    if pending:\\n                        future = completed.get()\\n                        name = pending.pop(future)\\n                        ready.extend([self._cancelled_result(name)] if future.cancelled() else outcomes.pop(name))\\n                except KeyboardInterrupt:\\n                    self.request_cancel()\\n            finished = True\\n        finally:\\n            if not finished:\\n                cancel.set()\\n            if executor is not None:\\n                self._shutdown(executor)\\n\\n    def _shutdown(self: Runner, executor: ThreadPoolExecutor) -> None:\\n        while True:\\n            try:\\n                executor.shutdown(wait=True, cancel_futures=True)\\n                return\\n            except KeyboardInterrupt:\\n                self.request_cancel()\\n\\n    def _cancelled_result(self: Runner, name: str) -> StepResult:\\n        with self._cancelled_lock:\\n            self._cancelled.add(name)\\n        return StepResult(name, success=False, message=\\\"cancelled\\\")\\n\\n    def plan(self: Runner, selected: list[tuple[str, BackupInstance]]) -> list[PlanEntry]:\\n        return self._plan(selected, datetime.now())\\n\\n    def _plan(self: Runner, selected: list[tuple[str, BackupInstance]], now: datetime) -> list[PlanEntry]:\\n        entries = []\\n        keys: list[str | None] = []\\n        outputs: dict[str, list[str]] = {}\\n        for name, instance in selected:\\n            capability = CAPABILITIES.get(instance.use)\\n            key: str | None = None\\n            if capability is None:  # pragma: no cover - load_config validates this\\n                entry = PlanEntry(name, \\\"\\\", \\\"\\\", \\\"fail\\\", f\\\"unknown capability '{instance.use}'\\\", False)\\n            else:\\n                call_kwargs: dict[str, object] = {\\n                    **instance.with_,\\n                    \\\"label\\\": name,\\n                    \\\"excludes\\\": list(effective_excludes(self._cfg, instance)),\\n                    \\\"now\\\": now,\\n                }\\n                try:\\n                    entry = capability.plan(**call_kwargs)\\n                except FatalError:\\n                    raise\\n                except Exception as exc:\\n                    # keep the configured output so duplicate detection still sees it\\n                    # (resolved with the run-wide timestamp, like the capability does)\\n                    configured = now.strftime(str(instance.with_.get(\\\"out\\\", \\\"\\\")))\\n                    entry = PlanEntry(name, \\\"\\\", configured, \\\"fail\\\", f\\\"{name} failed: {exc}\\\", False)\\n                if entry.out:\\n                    key = os.path.normpath(os.path.expanduser(entry.out))\\n                    outputs.setdefault(key, []).append(name)\\n            entries.append(entry)\\n            keys.append(key)\\n\\n        for index, (entry, out) in enumerate(zip(entries, keys, strict=True)):\\n            if out is not None and len(outputs[out]) > 1:\\n                names = outputs[out]\\n                others = \\\", \\\".join(sorted(name for name in names if name != entry.label))\\n                entries[index] = entry._replace(\\n                    out=out,\\n                    status=\\\"fail\\\",\\n                    reason=f\\\"output '{out}' is also the output of '{others}'\\\",\\n                )\\n        return entries\\n\\n    def _run_instance(\\n        self: Runner,\\n        name: str,\\n        instance: BackupInstance,\\n        now: datetime,\\n        cancel: threading.Event,\\n        outcomes: dict[str, list[StepResult]] | None = None,\\n    ) -> list[StepResult]:\\n        if outcomes is None:\\n            outcomes = {}\\n        if cancel.is_set():\\n            outcomes[name] = [self._cancelled_result(name)]\\n            return outcomes[name]\\n        capability = CAPABILITIES[instance.use]\\n\\n        call_kwargs: dict[str, object] = {\\n            **instance.with_,\\n            \\\"label\\\": name,\\n            \\\"excludes\\\": list(effective_excludes(self._cfg, instance)),\\n            \\\"dry_run\\\": self._dry_run,\\n            \\\"now\\\": now,\\n            \\\"cancel\\\": cancel,\\n        }\\n        try:\\n            outcomes[name] = list(capability.run(**call_kwargs))\\n        except ArchiveCancelled:\\n            outcomes[name] = [self._cancelled_result(name)]\\n        except Exception as exc:\\n            outcomes[name] = [StepResult(name, success=False, message=f\\\"{name} failed: {exc}\\\")]\\n        return outcomes[name]\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"69a981\",\"wall_time_seconds\":8.75e-7,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":3066,\"output\":\"# Changelog\\n\\nAll notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\\n\\nThe format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\\nand this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\\n\\n## [Unreleased]\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **backup**: archive engines report scanning, archiving and finalizing progress; Python archives include throttled input byte counts, and consumer errors do not stop backups.\\n- **backup**: immutable `ProgressEvent` records carry an instance label, phase and optional input byte counts.\\n- **backup**: `--jobs` controls concurrent archive instances (default three); cancellation stops new work, joins workers, stops tar and cleans temporary files while preserving completed results and backups; interruption exits with status 130.\\n- **backup**: `--plan` previews metadata-only run/skip/fail decisions, reasons and existing-output replacement markers; selected outputs share one timestamp and colliding paths are rejected before plan, run or dry-run execution.\\n- **backup**: optional `items` bundle literal source-relative files and directories into one archive, preserving empty directories and symlinks while applying inherited exclusions and `.bkpignore` rules.\\n- **backup**: per-instance `inherit_excludes`, `exclude_remove` and `exclude_add` control inherited exclusions for archives and `--show-excludes`.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **backup**: missing sources and bundles with no surviving members now warn and skip without touching previous archives; each run reports archived or previewed, skipped, and failed step counts. Existing non-directory sources and unreadable selected files still fail.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **backup**: interruption during submission or result collection preserves every instance's outcome and finishes cleanup without waiting for a lost completion notification.\\n- **backup**: a source or output metadata error while planning one instance now fails only that instance; the others still run, and duplicate outputs are still rejected.\\n- **backup**: `tar-archive` plans treat an empty `engine` as the default Python engine and report an empty `source` or `out` as \\\"no source configured\\\" or \\\"no out path configured\\\", the same as a run, instead of using the working directory; two instances with an empty `out` no longer report a false output collision.\\n- **sysup**: a `timeout` on a captured multi-command shell step now stops the whole step; before, only bash died and the command it was waiting on kept running. Such a step runs in its own process group, so Ctrl-C is forwarded to it, a terminal prompt it raises stops it until the timeout, and a command it runs as root through `sudo` is still out of reach and survives. Interactive steps and steps without a timeout are unchanged\\n\\n## [0.16.0] - 2026-10-29\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **sysup**: a step in a run entry, and a refresh command, may be written as a plain shell line such as `HOMEBREW_NO_UPGRADE_AUTO_UPDATES_CASKS=1 brew upgrade --quiet`, run by `bash -eo pipefail -c`, so variables, pipes, `&&` and `VAR=1 cmd` prefixes work as typed and a failing command anywhere in a pipe fails the step; an argv array still runs without a shell, and either may be an `{argv, env}` object whose `env` is merged over the parent environment for that step only (`~` expanded). An empty step is rejected when the config loads instead of failing mid-run\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **sysup**: the bundled entries are written as shell lines; `brew` upgrades with `HOMEBREW_NO_UPGRADE_AUTO_UPDATES_CASKS=1` and `--quiet`, leaving self-updating casks to their own updater; sudo priming triggers on any step that invokes `sudo`, not only one whose argv starts with it\\n\\n## [0.15.3] - 2026-10-28\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **sysup**: the bundled `brew upgrade` passes `--no-ask`, so Homebrew no longer stops the run with a \\\"Do you want to proceed with the upgrade?\\\" prompt now that the step is interactive\\n- **sysup**: the pipx-python step also walks `config.local.toml` beside the mise config, so pipx tools kept in the machine-local file follow the global python minor bump too\\n\\n## [0.15.2] - 2026-10-28\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **sysup**: the bundled `brew` step runs interactively, so the sudo password prompt Homebrew raises for casks is visible instead of hiding behind the spinner and freezing the run\\n\\n## [0.15.1] - 2026-10-27\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **sysup**: `uv-tool-python` now moves editable and otherwise customized uv tool installs with `uv tool upgrade --python`, so a host with an editable tool no longer ends every run red; only malformed receipts are skipped; tools already on the global minor are left alone, and a failed move restores the receipt uv deleted before rolling back\\n\\n## [0.15.0] - 2026-10-25\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **sysup**: `uv-tool-python` runs before uv upgrades on macOS, moving supported plain freestanding uv tools with older numeric Python pins or no pin to the global mise Python minor. Equal and newer pins stay unchanged. Failed moves attempt rollback and report recovery failures; customized or malformed receipts are visibly skipped untouched and may still fail during later uv upgrades.\\n- **dot**: `dot localize --shared <path>` opts a tracked file into a git clean/smudge filter instead of moving it: the file stays where it is, git stores the reserved `@HOME@` token in place of this host's home path, and each host's work tree gets its own home back on checkout. Combining `--shared` with `--to` is refused before any change. The attribute line is staged before the file is renormalized, so a failed share never leaves a tokenized file in the index without its attribute. The filter itself is hidden plumbing git calls (`dot filter clean|smudge <path>`): a pure byte transform that preserves line endings, BOM and non-UTF-8 content, and refuses any file that already contains `@HOME@`.\\n- **sysup**: a `sudo: true` flag on an entry (or any step starting with `sudo`) primes a cached sudo ticket before the run, refreshed in the background past sudo's 5-minute timeout; press Ctrl-D at the password prompt to skip priming (later steps then prompt for themselves as before), or Ctrl-C to abort the run entirely. A missing `sudo` binary or a declined prompt is not an error. Each run writes a per-run log under `$XDG_STATE_HOME/sysup/logs` (falling back to `~/.local/state/sysup/logs`), keeping the newest 10 files; a same-second filename collision gets a `-1`, `-2`, ... suffix. `sysup` prints the log path after every run that reaches one, clean, amber or red. A captured step's stdout/stderr lines starting with `error:` (case-insensitive) are flagged even on a zero exit code; up to 5 are printed inline, any more are summarized as `+N more, see log`. One entry producing any such lines reports a single `warned` result (amber, not red) instead of the usual success.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **sysup**: a `run:` entry now yields exactly one final `StepResult` for its whole `steps` list (success, failure, or `warned`), not one per step\\n- **sysup**: a run-log write failure now stops the run instead of continuing silently\\n\\n## [0.14.2] - 2026-10-17\\n\\n### Added\\n\\n- **sysup**: an `expiry:` registry of credentials with a hard expiry, checked before every ordinary run and fed into the attention summary, state, push and exit code: built-in `gh-token-expiry` (expiration header plus a private-repository grant probe; a disabled `gh-agent` template ships, enable it locally with your probe) and `x509-expiry` (PEM file or TLS endpoint, needs the new `sysup-cert` extra); commands can declare `needs: [item]` and are skipped with a `sysup refresh <item>` hint while the item is expired or unknown; `sysup refresh <item>` walks through renewal on a terminal, passes a pasted secret only on the stdin of the command that declares `stdin: secret`, and re-checks the item\\n- **dot**: `dot setup [URL]` brings up dotfiles on a fresh host: bare-clones the repo (URL argument or `BUVIS_DOT_REMOTE_URL`) into `<dotfiles_root>/.buvis`, hides untracked files, sets the upstream, checks out (refusing, never forcing, when existing files conflict, ignored ones included) and materializes host links and hooks. Every dot command now sets git `core.hooksPath` to `<dotfiles_root>/.config/buvis/hooks` when unset, never changing a set value, and `dot status` warns when the hooks dir or its `pre-commit` hook is missing.\\n- **sysup**: an end-of-run summary of everything unresolved, persisted in $XDG_STATE_HOME/sysup/attention.json until the failing command completes cleanly, pushed through ntfy (config `ntfy:` url/topic/credentials_file, env NTFY_URL/NTFY_TOPIC) once per run, and `sysup status` to read it back without running anything\\n- **dot**: `dot localize <target> [--to <source>]` command converts a tracked file to a host-specific link in one step: untrack, move to source location, create symlink, append to manifest, stage the manifest. `dot pull` now detects incoming deletions of host-link targets and rescues local bytes before the merge — moves become automatic, and collisions (both target and source exist with different content) prompt for resolution: keep-source (backup target), keep-target (backup old source), or cancel. The TUI (key `P`) shows a modal for each collision. Warning: raw `git pull` bypasses rescue — always use `dot pull` to protect host-specific files.\\n- **dot**: host-specific config links: a tracked `~/.config/buvis/host-links` manifest of `<target> => <source>` lines (both `$HOME`-relative); bad lines are reported as `host-links:<line>: <reason>` and skipped. New `dot materialize [--hook]` makes each target a link to its host-local source (a junction for directories on Windows); real files, wrong links, dangling links and missing sources are reported, never deleted or overwritten. Outside `--hook` it also installs missing `post-checkout`, `post-merge` and `post-rewrite` hooks into git `core.hooksPath` so links refresh after git operations; existing hook files are never changed. `dot status` lists link problems; the TUI status bar shows their count and `L` relinks.\\n\\n### Changed\\n\\n- **sysup**: exits 1 when a step failed during the run (was 0); \\\"expiring\\\" outcomes and entries retained from earlier runs keep exit 0\\n- **dot**: `dot pull` no longer fails with \\\"would be overwritten\\\" when an uncommitted edit touches a file the incoming commits change: it saves those edits to a `git apply`-able divergence patch in `~/.local/share/dot/divergence/`, resets the files to `HEAD` and lets upstream win. A file with both staged and unstaged changes stops the pull untouched. `dot status` and the TUI status bar warn while a patch is pending.\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **dot**: pull no longer overwrites a locally edited git-secret file whose upstream copy changed; the edit is saved encrypted in the divergence patch\\n- **dot**: `dot add <file>` tracks a file the repository has never seen instead of answering `No changes.`; a path relative to the current directory resolves there before the dotfiles root; an ignored file is refused with the matching rule and the `git add -f` escape; paths are shell-quoted and matched literally, so `file[1].txt` no longer also stages `file1.txt`; a path that exists nowhere is an error instead of a fall-through to the whole-tree picker; afterwards the command prints what is currently staged for the path, or warns when nothing is.\\n- **sysup**: the mise step updates mise itself without a confirmation prompt, which sysup could never answer, so it no longer fails with \\\"self-update cancelled\\\".\\n- **pybase**: config merge no longer mutates an earlier layer's nested dict when a later layer overrides the same key.\\n- **pybase**: auto-update asks the installer's index (mise, uv, pip) what it can install, not PyPI, so a lagging mirror or release-age gate no longer costs a useless upgrade and restart on every run. An upgrade that installs nothing is logged as an error and skipped for that version for 24 hours. `--update` says when PyPI is ahead of the installer's index and fails when the upgrade did not apply.\\n\\n## [0.14.1] - 2026-10-12\\n\\n### Fixed\\n\\n- **pybase**: stop every CLI hanging in an endless silent restart loop when the installer reports success without installing (e.g. mise hides releases younger than its `minimum_release_age`); the auto-update now runs at most once per invocation.\\n\\n\"}}"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"status\":\"fulfilled\",\"value\":{\"chunk_id\":\"ed5679\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.00000175,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":771,\"output\":\"---\\nname: python-patterns\\ndescription: Use when writing, reviewing, or refactoring Python code. Covers idioms, naming, traps, toolchain, and security. Triggers on Python file edits, \\\"pythonic\\\", \\\"PEP 8\\\", \\\"python best practices\\\", \\\"python idioms\\\", \\\"bandit\\\".\\ncompatibility: \\\"Portable standalone compatibility copy of the strunk plugin skill for agents without that plugin; Claude Code uses the namespaced plugin skill.\\\"\\n---\\n\\n# Python Patterns\\n\\nPick a side. These are the rulings, not a tutorial.\\n\\n## Idioms that earn their place\\n\\n| Rule | Why |\\n|------|-----|\\n| `X \\\\| Y`, not `Union[X, Y]` | 3.10+ runtime syntax; clearer. On 3.9 it parses only under `from __future__ import annotations` and still breaks `get_type_hints`/`isinstance` — keep `Optional`/`Union` if you support 3.9. |\\n| `Protocol`, not `ABC`, for duck typing | Structural typing; no inheritance you don't control. |\\n| `@dataclass(frozen=True)` for immutable records, plain `dataclass` only when you must mutate, `NamedTuple` for lightweight immutable rows | Pydantic only when you need validation. |\\n| `raise New(...) from e` | Marks the explicit cause. A bare `raise New()` still chains implicitly (\\\"During handling...\\\"); use `from None` to suppress. |\\n| `@functools.wraps` on every decorator | Without it the wrapper loses `__name__`, `__doc__`, signature. |\\n| `pathlib.Path`, not `os.path` | One object, no string surgery. |\\n\\n## Naming (PEP 8)\\n\\n`snake_case` for modules, functions, variables. `CapWords` for classes. `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE` for constants. Leading `_` for internal names.\\n\\n**Never name a `.py` file in kebab-case.** `my-script.py` is not importable (`import my-script` is a `SyntaxError`). Kebab-case belongs only in the `pyproject.toml` distribution name and the CLI command, both of which map to a `snake_case` module.\\n\\n## Traps\\n\\n```python\\n# Mutable default argument — shared across calls\\ndef bad(items=[]): ...\\ndef good(items=None):\\n    items = [] if items is None else items\\n\\n# Bare except — swallows SystemExit, KeyboardInterrupt\\ntry: ...\\nexcept Exception as e: ...        # name the exception\\n\\n# __slots__ kills __dict__ and dynamic attributes — use only on fixed,\\n# memory-sensitive classes you won't extend.\\n\\n# __double_leading is name-mangled to _Class__double_leading.\\n# Surprises you in subclasses, pickling, and monkey-patching.\\n\\nif value is None: ...             # never == None\\n```\\n\\nAvoid `from module import *`. Use `isinstance`, never `type(x) ==`.\\n\\n## Toolchain\\n\\n**black** to format, **isort** for imports, **ruff** to lint. Type annotations on every function signature — no exceptions for \\\"obvious\\\" returns.\\n\\n## Resources and laziness\\n\\nContext managers (`with`) for anything holding a resource. Generators for lazy iteration over anything you would otherwise materialize into a list.\\n\\n## Security\\n\\n- Read secrets with `os.environ[\\\"API_KEY\\\"]`, never `.get()` — a missing secret must raise `KeyError` at startup, not surface as `None` at request time.\\n- `python-dotenv` loads a local `.env` in development only. Never commit one.\\n- `bandit -r src/` for static security analysis.\\n\"}}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a198fd-33f1-70c2-89d9-ea1eb12b98ca","create_time":1793568109.227603}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
