feature.\nExisting public configuration declarations:\nclass BackupInstance(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"One configured backup capability instance.\n\n    ``use`` names a registered capability, ``with:`` supplies its inputs, and\n    ``tags`` group instances for ``--tag`` selection. ``enabled: false`` skips\n    the instance without deleting it.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\", frozen=True, populate_by_name=True)\n\n    use: str\n    enabled: bool = True\n    order: int = 100\n    tags: tuple[str, ...] = ()\n    inherit_excludes: bool = True\n    exclude_add: tuple[str, ...] = ()\n    exclude_remove: tuple[str, ...] = ()\n    with_: dict[str, object] = Field(default_factory=dict, alias=\"with\")\n\n\nclass BackupConfig(BaseModel):\n    \"\"\"The whole merged, validated backup configuration.\n\n    ``instances`` is a name-keyed map of capability instances; ``excludes`` is\n    the global basename exclude list that layers via 00080's ``excludes+`` /\n    ``excludes-`` directives across gem -> user -> machine config.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\", frozen=True)\n\n    instances: dict[str, BackupInstance] = {}\n    excludes: tuple[str, ...] = ()\n\nStepResult(label: str, success: bool, message: str = \"\", archive: ArchiveMeta | None = None, *, skipped: bool = False). ArchiveMeta(out_path: str, file_count: int, total_bytes: int). PlanEntry(label: str, source: str, out: str, status: str, reason: str, replaces: bool). ProgressEvent(label, phase, done_bytes, total_bytes).\n\nCapability protocol: inputs property Mapping[str, object]; plan(**kwargs: object) -> PlanEntry; run(**kwargs: object) -> Iterator[StepResult]. Registry backup.capabilities.CAPABILITIES: dict[str, Capability], initially tar-archive. Controlled fake capabilities at this public registry boundary are permitted; never mock Runner itself for runner tests.\n\n\nTest framework: pytest; uv run pytest tests/tools/backup/test_progress.py -q --tb=line; class-based tests; Python 3.11+\n\nRules:\n1. Write tests from requirements only. You are a USER of this API, not its builder.\n2. Each test name describes a behavior: \"rejects empty email\", \"returns 404 for missing user\"\n3. Assert on outputs, return values, side effects, and errors. Never assert on mock existence.\n4. Cover edge cases: empty input, null/undefined, boundary values, error conditions\n5. Use real code paths. Mock only external dependencies (network, filesystem, databases).\n6. For EACH test, ask yourself: \"What wrong implementation would still pass this?\" If easy to answer, add constraints.\n6a. DEVON-PROOF: for every function you test, include all six: (1) two distinct fixture values per asserted field, so a hardcoded value fails; (2) a near-miss negative that shares a prefix, suffix or substring with a positive and must not match, so substring, prefix and basename matching fail; (3) an input with more than one element and an empty input, so first-element, last-element and truncation shortcuts fail; (4) for any guard, filter or classifier, an accept and a reject case of the same shape, so a constant return fails; (5) an error case that asserts the exception or failure signal is surfaced, so a swallowed error or fail-open passes nothing; (6) a dependency assertion that a cwd, environment value or passed argument reached the call or changed a side effect, so dropped plumbing fails. Devon will try exactly these; a draft that lacks one is sent back to you.\n7. Do not write implementation code. Do not modify non-test files.\n8. Do not add test-only methods to production classes. Use test utilities instead.\n9. SIMPLICITY FIRST: Test only the behavior in the acceptance criteria. Do not add tests for features nobody asked for. If you write 20 tests when 6 cover the spec, cut back.\n10. THINK BEFORE CODING: If the acceptance criteria are ambiguous (unclear input shape, unstated error behavior, multiple valid interpretations), STOP and report the ambiguity in