+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:53:42.723Z","ordinal":24,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1991a-7acb-7391-a53a-65f225e626c8","turn_id":"01a1991a-7af9-7e23-98e0-a1374fe671a7","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-c95d1255-ae99-497a-ac43-cb1b425f99a9","process_id":"71156","command":["/opt/homebrew/bin/bash","-lc","python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot = Path('/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems')\nfor parent in ('docs', 'docs/source', 'docs/source/tools'):\n    p = root / parent / 'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'INSTRUCTIONS {p.relative_to(root)}\\n{p.read_text()}')\nfor name, start, end in [('README.md', 110, 120), ('docs/source/tools/backup.rst', 1, 65), ('CHANGELOG.md', 1, 30)]:\n    lines = (root / name).read_text().splitlines()\n    print(f'FILE {name}:{start}-{end}')\n    for number in range(start, min(end, len(lines)) + 1):\n        print(f'{number}: {lines[number - 1]}')\nPY"],"cwd":"file:///Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"python - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nroot = Path('/Users/bob/git/src/github.com/buvis/gems')\nfor parent in ('docs', 'docs/source', 'docs/source/tools'):\n    p = root / parent / 'AGENTS.md'\n    if p.is_file():\n        print(f'INSTRUCTIONS {p.relative_to(root)}\\n{p.read_text()}')\nfor name, start, end in [('README.md', 110, 120), ('docs/source/tools/backup.rst', 1, 65), ('CHANGELOG.md', 1, 30)]:\n    lines = (root / name).read_text().splitlines()\n    print(f'FILE {name}:{start}-{end}')\n    for number in range(start, min(end, len(lines)) + 1):\n        print(f'{number}: {lines[number - 1]}')\nPY"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","aggregated_output":"FILE README.md:110-120\n110: ### backup\n111: \n112: `--jobs N` runs up to N instances concurrently (default: 3, minimum: 1).\n113: Use `--jobs 1` for sequential execution. Instances are submitted by configured order,\n114: then name; results appear in completion order.\n115: \n116: Ctrl-C stops new jobs, cancels and joins active writers, preserves completed\n117: and prior archives, and exits with status 130.\n118: \n119: ### dot host-specific config\n120: \nFILE docs/source/tools/backup.rst:1-65\n1: .. _tool-backup:\n2: \n3: backup\n4: ======\n5: \n6: Config-driven archiver that wraps the git-src backup as a ``tar-archive``\n7: capability. What gets archived — which source trees, where the archive lands,\n8: and what is excluded — is defined in configuration, not code, so adding or\n9: retuning a backup is a config edit rather than a release.\n10: \n11: Usage\n12: -----\n13: \n14: .. code-block:: bash\n15: \n16:     backup                              # run enabled instances with up to 3 jobs\n17:     backup --jobs 1                     # run instances strictly sequentially\n18:     backup --only git-src,docs          # run only these instances (comma list or repeated)\n19:     backup --tag nightly                # run only instances carrying this tag\n20:     backup --list                       # print the configured instances and exit\n21:     backup --plan                       # report run/skip/fail decisions from metadata only\n22:     backup --dry-run                    # walk and report what would be archived, write nothing\n23:     backup --only git-src --source DIR --out FILE   # one-shot override of the selected instance\n24:     backup --show-excludes git-src --for PATH       # print the resolved excludes under PATH\n25: \n26: ``--source`` / ``--out`` override the single selected instance for one run (a\n27: non-empty flag wins over the configured value); they require exactly one\n28: selected instance, via ``--only``. ``--show-excludes`` is read-only: it prints\n29: the resolved exclude set and, with ``--for <path>``, the ``.bkpignore`` rules\n30: effective under that path, then exits without writing an archive.\n31: \n32: Parallel execution\n33: ------------------\n34: \n35: ``--jobs N`` limits concurrent instances (default 3; integer, at least 1).\n36: ``--jobs 1`` runs strictly sequentially. Instances are submitted in ascending\n37: ``(order, name)`` order; results appear as instances complete. An instance does\n38: not wait for every lower-order instance to finish before it can start.\n39: \n40: Ctrl-C stops new work, requests cancellation of active writers and waits for\n41: them to stop. The command names unfinished instances and exits with status 130.\n42: Completed archives remain available; cancellation before publication preserves\n43: any previous archive. Python archive cancellation takes effect after the\n44: current member finishes. Parallel execution does not guarantee a speedup.\n45: \n46: Planning\n47: --------\n48: \n49: ``--plan`` reports a metadata-only preview with a ``run``, ``skip`` or ``fail``\n50: row for each selected instance, including the reason and a replacement marker\n51: when its output already exists. It does not write archives or walk their\n52: contents. Use ``--only`` and ``--tag`` to select instances; ``--source`` and\n53: ``--out`` apply the same single-instance overrides as a normal run. A plan with\n54: any ``fail`` row exits with status 1. ``--plan`` and ``--dry-run`` are mutually\n55: exclusive; use ``--dry-run`` to walk and preview archive members.\n56: \n57: All selected output paths use one timestamp for ``strftime`` expansion.\n58: Colliding output paths are rejected for plans, normal runs and dry runs after\n59: lexical ``expanduser`` / ``normpath`` normalization; this check does not resolve\n60: symlinks. A plan is a point-in-time preview: execution rechecks conditions before\n61: writing, so later filesystem changes can alter the outcome.\n62: \n63: Configuration\n64: -------------\n65: \nFILE CHANGELOG.md:1-30\n1: # Changelog\n2: \n3: All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\n4: \n5: The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\n6: and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\n7: \n8: ## [Unreleased]\n9: \n10: ### Added\n11: \n12: - **backup**: interactive runs show per-instance live progress and elapsed time; `--quiet` hides redraws while preserving final results and summary counts.\n13: - **backup**: the runner delivers bounded, cancellation-aware progress and idle ticks on the calling thread before terminal results.\n14: - **backup**: archive engines report scanning, archiving and finalizing progress; Python archives include throttled input byte counts, and consumer errors do not stop backups.\n15: - **backup**: immutable `ProgressEvent` records carry an instance label, phase and optional input byte counts.\n16: - **backup**: `--jobs` controls concurrent archive instances (default three); cancellation stops new work, joins workers, stops tar and cleans temporary files while preserving completed results and backups; interruption exits with status 130.\n17: - **backup**: `--plan` previews metadata-only run/skip/fail decisions, reasons and existing-output replacement markers; selected outputs share one timestamp and colliding paths are rejected before plan, run or dry-run execution.\n18: - **backup**: optional `items` bundle literal source-relative files and directories into one archive, preserving empty directories and symlinks while applying inherited exclusions and `.bkpignore` rules.\n19: - **backup**: per-instance `inherit_excludes`, `exclude_remove` and `exclude_add` control inherited exclusions for archives and `--show-excludes`.\n20: \n21: ### Changed\n22: \n23: - **backup**: missing sources and bundles with no surviving members now warn and skip without touching previous archives; each run reports archived or previewed, skipped, and failed step counts. Existing non-directory sources and unreadable selected files still fail.\n24: \n25: ### Fixed\n26: \n27: - **backup**: interruption during submission or result collection preserves every instance's outcome and finishes cleanup without waiting for a lost completion notification.\n28: - **backup**: a source or output metadata error while planning one instance now fails only that instance; the others still run, and duplicate outputs are still rejected.\n29: - **backup**: `tar-archive` plans treat an empty `engine` as the default Python engine and report an empty `source` or `out` as \"no source configured\" or \"no out path configured\", the same as a run, instead of using the working directory; two instances with an empty `out` no longer report a false output collision.\n30: - **sysup**: a `timeout` on a captured multi-command shell step now stops the whole step; before, only bash died and the command it was waiting on kept running. Such a step runs in its own process group, so Ctrl-C is forwarded to it, a terminal prompt it raises stops it until the timeout, and a command it runs as root through `sudo` is still out of reach and survives. Interactive steps and steps without a timeout are unchanged\n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":2500}},"started_at_ms":1793570022723,"completed_at_ms":1793570022723}}
+{"timestamp":"2026-11-01T21:53:42.725Z","ordinal":25,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a1991a-a545-7943-b6af-d22716c831a2","call_id":"call_883f32ee233b4ba5818a4f5a41431f34","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"97e21d\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.0000025,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":1788,\"output\":\"FILE README.md:110-120\\n110: ### backup\\n111: \\n112: `--jobs N` runs up to N instances concurrently (default: 3, minimum: 1).\\n113: Use `--jobs 1` for sequential execution. Instances are submitted by configured order,\\n114: then name; results appear in completion order.\\n115: \\n116: Ctrl-C stops new jobs, cancels and joins active writers, preserves completed\\n117: and prior archives, and exits with status 130.\\n118: \\n119: ### dot host-specific config\\n120: \\nFILE docs/source/tools/backup.rst:1-65\\n1: .. _tool-backup:\\n2: \\n3: backup\\n4: ======\\n5: \\n6: Config-driven archiver that wraps the git-src backup as a ``tar-archive``\\n7: capability. What gets archived — which source trees, where the archive lands,\\n8: and what is excluded — is defined in configuration, not code, so adding or\\n9: retuning a backup is a config edit rather than a release.\\n10: \\n11: Usage\\n12: -----\\n13: \\n14: .. code-block:: bash\\n15: \\n16:     backup                              # run enabled instances with up to 3 jobs\\n17:     backup --jobs 1                     # run instances strictly sequentially\\n18:     backup --only git-src,docs          # run only these instances (comma list or repeated)\\n19:     backup --tag nightly                # run only instances carrying this tag\\n20:     backup --list                       # print the configured instances and exit\\n21:     backup --plan                       # report run/skip/fail decisions from metadata only\\n22:     backup --dry-run                    # walk and report what would be archived, write nothing\\n23:     backup --only git-src --source DIR --out FILE   # one-shot override of the selected instance\\n24:     backup --show-excludes git-src --for PATH       # print the resolved excludes under PATH\\n25: \\n26: ``--source`` / ``--out`` override the single selected instance for one run (a\\n27: non-empty flag wins over the configured value); they require exactly one\\n28: selected instance, via ``--only``. ``--show-excludes`` is read-only: it prints\\n29: the resolved exclude set and, with ``--for <path>``, the ``.bkpignore`` rules\\n30: effective under that path, then exits without writing an archive.\\n31: \\n32: Parallel execution\\n33: ------------------\\n34: \\n35: ``--jobs N`` limits concurrent instances (default 3; integer, at least 1).\\n36: ``--jobs 1`` runs strictly sequentially. Instances are submitted in ascending\\n37: ``(order, name)`` order; results appear as instances complete. An instance does\\n38: not wait for every lower-order instance to finish before it can start.\\n39: \\n40: Ctrl-C stops new work, requests cancellation of active writers and waits for\\n41: them to stop. The command names unfinished instances and exits with status 130.\\n42: Completed archives remain available; cancellation before publication preserves\\n43: any previous archive. Python archive cancellation takes effect after the\\n44: current member finishes. Parallel execution does not guarantee a speedup.\\n45: \\n46: Planning\\n47: --------\\n48: \\n49: ``--plan`` reports a metadata-only preview with a ``run``, ``skip`` or ``fail``\\n50: row for each selected instance, including the reason and a replacement marker\\n51: when its output already exists. It does not write archives or walk their\\n52: contents. Use ``--only`` and ``--tag`` to select instances; ``--source`` and\\n53: ``--out`` apply the same single-instance overrides as a normal run. A plan with\\n54: any ``fail`` row exits with status 1. ``--plan`` and ``--dry-run`` are mutually\\n55: exclusive; use ``--dry-run`` to walk and preview archive members.\\n56: \\n57: All selected output paths use one timestamp for ``strftime`` expansion.\\n58: Colliding output paths are rejected for plans, normal runs and dry runs after\\n59: lexical ``expanduser`` / ``normpath`` normalization; this check does not resolve\\n60: symlinks. A plan is a point-in-time preview: execution rechecks conditions before\\n61: writing, so later filesystem changes can alter the outcome.\\n62: \\n63: Configuration\\n64: -------------\\n65: \\nFILE CHANGELOG.md:1-30\\n1: # Changelog\\n2: \\n3: All notable changes to this project will be documented in this file.\\n4: \\n5: The format is based on [Keep a Changelog](https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/),\\n6: and this project adheres to [Semantic Versioning](https://semver.org/spec/v2.0.0.html).\\n7: \\n8: ## [Unreleased]\\n9: \\n10: ### Added\\n11: \\n12: - **backup**: interactive runs show per-instance live progress and elapsed time; `--quiet` hides redraws while preserving final results and summary counts.\\n13: - **backup**: the runner delivers bounded, cancellation-aware progress and idle ticks on the calling thread before terminal results.\\n14: - **backup**: archive engines report scanning, archiving and finalizing progress; Python archives include throttled input byte counts, and consumer errors do not stop backups.\\n15: - **backup**: immutable `ProgressEvent` records carry an instance label, phase and optional input byte counts.\\n16: - **backup**: `--jobs` controls concurrent archive instances (default three); cancellation stops new work, joins workers, stops tar and cleans temporary files while preserving completed results and backups; interruption exits with status 130.\\n17: - **backup**: `--plan` previews metadata-only run/skip/fail decisions, reasons and existing-output replacement markers; selected outputs share one timestamp and colliding paths are rejected before plan, run or dry-run execution.\\n18: - **backup**: optional `items` bundle literal source-relative files and directories into one archive, preserving empty directories and symlinks while applying inherited exclusions and `.bkpignore` rules.\\n19: - **backup**: per-instance `inherit_excludes`, `exclude_remove` and `exclude_add` control inherited exclusions for archives and `--show-excludes`.\\n20: \\n21: ### Changed\\n22: \\n23: - **backup**: missing sources and bundles with no surviving members now warn and skip without touching previous archives; each run reports archived or previewed, skipped, and failed step counts. Existing non-directory sources and unreadable selected files still fail.\\n24: \\n25: ### Fixed\\n26: \\n27: - **backup**: interruption during submission or result collection preserves every instance's outcome and finishes cleanup without waiting for a lost completion notification.\\n28: - **backup**: a source or output metadata error while planning one instance now fails only that instance; the others still run, and duplicate outputs are still rejected.\\n29: - **backup**: `tar-archive` plans treat an empty `engine` as the default Python engine and report an empty `source` or `out` as \\\"no source configured\\\" or \\\"no out path configured\\\", the same as a run, instead of using the working directory; two instances with an empty `out` no longer report a false output collision.\\n30: - **sysup**: a `timeout` on a captured multi-command shell step now stops the whole step; before, only bash died and the command it was waiting on kept running. Such a step runs in its own process group, so Ctrl-C is forwarded to it, a terminal prompt it raises stops it until the timeout, and a command it runs as root through `sudo` is still out of reach and survives. Interactive steps and steps without a timeout are unchanged\\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a1991a-7af9-7e23-98e0-a1374fe671a7","create_time":1793570022.72591}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
